"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Email as an attack path': {
        'body': '\nPeople already open mail for payroll, vendors, and ordinary work, so a message that reaches that inbox can steer a real decision. The path is the mailbox channel. The wording inside might be a fake sign-in page, a harmful attachment, or a later demand to move money, and those tricks have their own names. What puts the case here is that the request was delivered as mail. A display name the reader recognizes is only a label. The envelope sender is a different field, and a gateway can accept a note whose label says Payroll while the address sits on a domain the company never bought.\n\nServer-to-server transfer still uses port 25. A person sending from a mail program usually submits on port 587. SPF asks whether that sending address is allowed for the envelope domain. DKIM signs selected headers and the body so a quiet edit in transit can be spotted. DMARC checks whether those results line up with the visible From domain and can order a reject, a quarantine, or only a report. A lookalike domain can pass all three tests for itself. A pass proves the sender controls the lookalike, not that the brand is real. The address that will open is stored in the link target, and it can disagree with the friendly words on the screen. Macro spreadsheets and archives are the other usual payload, and they stay inert until someone opens them.\n\nPriya, a payroll clerk at a neighborhood clinic, got a message with the subject line about a direct-deposit change. The display name read Benefits Desk. The envelope domain swapped letters for digits so it resembled the clinic name at a glance. The gateway log showed a DMARC failure for the real clinic domain, a pass for the lookalike, and a rule set to tag rather than quarantine, so the message still landed in her inbox. The blue words said clinic portal. The link target was a host the clinic does not own. She sent the original message, headers included, to the security mailbox and did not type her password.\n\nA defender copies Received hops, Reply-To, Return-Path, and the link host into the ticket before anyone signs in. Quarantine on DMARC failure, a block on internet macros, and rewritten links make the bad message harder to act on. Staff should reach payroll from a bookmark they already trust. Mail remains a normal business tool, and the attack is the abuse of the trust people place in it.\n\nExam questions often swap the path for the trick that rode on it. If the stem asks where the lure arrived, the mailbox is the answer. If the stem asks what the words were trying to get, the answer may be a phish, or a payment fraud when the goal is a new bank account. A short phone text and a chat line from a coworker are neighboring paths, even when the sentences sound alike. A fully patched laptop does not close this path. The weakness is the decision to trust the destination, not a missing operating-system fix.\n',
        'r0': '\nMail between servers still uses port 25, and a person sending from a client usually submits on port 587. SPF checks the envelope domain against the addresses allowed to send for it. DKIM signs selected headers and the body. DMARC looks for alignment with the visible From domain and can reject or quarantine failures. A lookalike domain can pass SPF, DKIM, and DMARC for itself, so a pass does not prove the message is from payroll. The host that will open lives in the link target and can differ from the words on the screen. Macro documents and archives are inert until someone opens them and allows active content. Copy Return-Path, Reply-To, and the Received hops into the ticket before anyone follows the link.\n',
        'r1': '\nPeople trust the inbox, so attackers send lures there. The visible name can be a lie. The real sender can be a lookalike domain. Friendly words can hide a different link. A harmful file can ride along too. Macros do nothing until the file is opened. A filter can tag mail and still deliver it. Save the original so the headers remain. Check the link host before any sign-in. Open payroll from a bookmark, not the message. A phone text is a different path. A coworker chat is a different path. Patching the laptop does not close the inbox. The attack abuses trust in ordinary mail.\n',
        'r2': "\nAn apartment building has one mail slot for each unit, and most of what comes through is expected. Bills, a note from the manager, and a card from a relative all use that same slot. One envelope copies the manager's nickname and asks the tenant to write the door code on a card that goes to a different street. The paper does not look wild. It arrived beside the electric bill, which is why it almost works. Neighbors even say they always answer notes from that slot. That habit is the whole trick. The work inbox behaves the same way. A message there can ask someone to type a password on a page the company does not own, and the familiar mailbox is why the request feels routine.\n",
        'r4': '\nSave the original message so the header block stays with the ticket. Write down the link host before anyone types a password. Compare that host with the payroll bookmark the staff already use. Leave the message unopened on any machine that holds an admin session. Keep internet macros blocked in the mail client and the office suite while you investigate. Note the display name, the envelope sender, and whether the gateway tagged the message or held it.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to name the deception and forget the channel, or to name the channel when the question asks what the wording was for. A mailbox message is this path. A phone text and a chat line are the neighbors, and the artifact in the ticket decides which one you have. A demand to change a bank account can ride the same inbox, but that money pattern is a further label. Do not call the message a flaw with no vendor fix unless it abuses a bug that still has no patch.\n',
    },
    'Text messages as an attack path': {
        'body': '\nA lock-screen text is built to be acted on in a few seconds. Carriers, banks, and delivery firms already send real notes, so a tap feels ordinary. That habit is the path. The sender label on a text is easy to forge, and a five-digit or six-digit short code can be rented for a week by someone who does not own the brand. The bubble stays short so the reader pays a fee or opens a link before comparing it with the carrier app already on the phone.\n\nThis channel is not the company mailbox and not a chat thread in a work tool, even if someone later pastes the same words into either place. When a question asks what kind of lie the bubble told, the technique has its own name. When it asks where the lure appeared, the answer stays with the handset message. Shortened links hide the real host until a preview expands them, and that preview must not sign the person in. A follow-up line may ask for a six-digit code that a real bank or mail login just generated. The phone is then both the bait and the place the second factor is displayed.\n\nLuis had no parcel in transit. At 12:14 his phone showed a text from the short code 55421 saying a package was held and a fee was due before evening. He did not tap. The carrier app, opened from its icon, listed no held package. He photographed the sender field and the full link, then handed the phone to the service desk. Expanded later on a machine that was not signed into clinic mail, the host was one letter off the real carrier and the domain had been registered that morning.\n\nPeople should open the carrier, the bank, or the toll account from the installed app or from an address they type themselves. The text does not get to supply that address. A screenshot of the sender and the link is the report, not a visit to the page from a phone that is already inside company mail. The clinic mail gateway never sees the bubble, so a strict mail policy does not stop it. A warning about a brand-new host is useful and still leaves the decision with the person. The attack is the urgent text. Refusing the tap, and checking the real app, is the defense.\n\nA short link tempts students to name only a near-spell domain and to skip the channel that carried it. Mail is the wrong neighbor when the artifact is a handset bubble, even if both stories mention a package. A voice call a minute later is a second event and needs its own note. The bubble remains this path. A current phone does not remove it, because the messaging app is supposed to show texts from outside, and the weak point is a sender field that was never strong proof of who sent it.\n',
        'r0': '\nSMS labels can be spoofed, and a five-digit or six-digit short code can be leased so the bubble looks like a brand the person already trusts. Shortened links hide the real domain until a preview that does not sign anyone in expands them. The path is the handset message. The deceptive technique that often rides on it has its own name, and a stem can ask for the channel or for the lie. A second step may ask the reader to forward a six-digit one-time code a real login just created. SPF, DKIM, and DMARC never see the bubble, because it does not pass through the mail gateway. Ticket facts are the sender string, the expanded host, and whether any real parcel, toll, or bank event existed that day.\n',
        'r1': '\nA text on a phone can be a trap. The sender name is easy to fake. A short numeric code can be rented. Parcel and bank notes train people to tap. A shortened link hides the real site. Do not sign in from that text. Open the real app and check there. Do not forward a code that just arrived. Mail filters never see this bubble. Photograph the sender and the link. A mailbox lure is a different path. A work chat is a different path. A new phone still gets bad texts. The attack is the urgent message itself.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic fridge collects sticky notes all morning. Most of them are real: a patient running late, a lab that needs a call back. One note, written in marker, says a parcel fee is due and scribbles a tiny web address the clinic has never used. It is short enough that nobody asks a second question. The nickname even matches the delivery firm the clinic uses every week. Staff are supposed to look at the tracking page they already bookmarked, not at an address copied off a scrap. The scrap is the trap and the bookmark is the check. A handset text works the same way. The bubble is brief, the sender can be borrowed, and the only safe tracking page is the one opened from the real carrier app.\n',
        'r4': '\nPhotograph the sender field and the full link before anyone deletes the text. Expand a shortened link only in a preview that does not sign anyone in, and write that host in the ticket. Open the carrier or bank app from its icon and record whether a real parcel or bill exists. Do not type a one-time code into any page the text suggested. Leave the company mail session closed on that phone until the host is known. Do not paste the bubble into work chat, or the link spreads onto a second channel.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to name only the deceptive technique, or only a near-spell domain, when the stem shows a handset screenshot and asks for the path. Mail is the wrong neighbor when the artifact is the bubble, even if the story mentions a package. A call that follows is a second event. The text still counts. Mail-gateway checks are the wrong story, because this message never touched that gateway. A current phone does not close the path.\n',
    },
    'Instant messages as an attack path': {
        'body': "\nChat asks for a reply while the other person still looks present. People answer a green dot faster than they answer mail, and that speed is the path. The dangerous case is often a real account, not a stranger with a new display name. If a coworker loses a phone, the chat session on that phone can keep speaking. The photo stays authentic because it belongs to the real account. The words after the theft are the attacker's. A thread history above the new line makes the request look like a continuation of ordinary work.\n\nNora and Luis share a long thread about clinic scheduling. Luis reported his phone stolen at 8:10. At 8:16 that same chat account asked Nora for the six-digit VPN code showing on her handset. She did not type it. The roster still showed him as active, which only meant a session was connected, not that he was at a keyboard. Nora walked to his empty desk and called the extension printed on his nameplate. He answered from the lobby on a borrowed handset and had sent nothing. The six-minute gap between the theft report and the code request is the fact that belongs in the ticket, next to the transcript.\n\nThe messenger is the path, whether it is the company chat product or a personal app a coworker linked to work. A file or a link dropped in the same window can hurt as soon as it is opened, much as an attachment can. Unreviewed plugins and screen-sharing add-ins widen the damage, because a taken-over account can then steer the other person's machine. Passwords, one-time codes, and payment changes do not belong in a chat reply, no matter how familiar the name looks. The check has to use a channel the thief does not hold: a desk visit, a known voice number, or an in-person question.\n\nDefenders write a plain rule that codes and payment changes are never finished inside chat, and they back the person who refuses. The ticket should hold the transcript, the clock times, and which second channel confirmed or denied the ask. Signing the stolen phone out of chat, and revoking its session, is containment of a live account. It is not the same step as wiping a malware file after evidence is saved. The attack is the request sent from the taken-over account. Counting the green dot as proof of identity is exactly what that attack needs from the reader.\n\nStudents file every short deceptive note as mail, or they call this one a phone text because it is brief. The artifact is the chat transcript, so the path is the messenger. The lie may also be impersonation of Luis, and that second label does not erase the channel. A stolen mailbox that demands a wire transfer is a different pattern aimed at money. Here the ask was a VPN code inside an existing thread minutes after a phone-theft report. A fully updated chat app still delivers messages from sessions that remain signed in. The weakness is the stolen session plus the habit of answering before you leave your chair.\n",
        'r0': '\nA taken-over chat session keeps the real display name and photo, so the transcript above the new line can be genuine while the new line is not. In the clinic case the phone-theft report was logged at 8:10 and the VPN-code request arrived at 8:16, a six-minute gap and a six-digit code. Presence on the roster only shows that a session is connected. Verification belongs on a second channel the thief does not hold, such as a desk phone on a known extension. Revoking that session is containment of the stolen login. Company chat and a linked personal messenger are both this path if the artifact is the chat line rather than a mailbox or an SMS bubble.\n',
        'r1': '\nPeople answer chat faster than they answer mail. A stolen phone can keep the chat session. The photo and the name can both be real. The new line can still be the attacker. A green dot is not a person at the keyboard. Do not type a code back into that chat. Walk over or call a known number. A link in the chat can still be harmful. Plugins can let the account steer a screen. Signing out the stolen phone cuts the live session. A text bubble is a different path. A mailbox note is a different path.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic hallway has a whiteboard where nurses leave short notes for the next shift. Most notes are real, written by people who were just there. One afternoon a note appears in a familiar handwriting asking for the medication-room code, minutes after that nurse's badge was reported missing. The board looks the same as every other day. Coworkers are used to answering it without walking down the hall. The right move is to find the nurse in person before anyone reads a code aloud. Speed and a familiar hand are the pressure. Work chat behaves the same way. A trusted thread can carry a stranger's request as soon as the signed-in session is stolen.\n",
        'r4': '\nSave the chat transcript and write the theft-report time next to the request time. Do not reply with a code, a password, or a payment change inside that thread. Reach the coworker on a desk phone or in person and record what they say. Sign the stolen device out of chat and leave other sessions in place until you know which ones are still theirs. Note whether a file or a link arrived in the same line. Do not install a chat add-in the sender just recommended.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call a short chat line a phone text, or to call it ordinary mail, because the words could have been pasted into either channel. The transcript is the artifact, so the path is the messenger. Impersonation may also fit the lie, and it does not replace the channel. A wire-transfer demand from a mailbox is a different money pattern. Revoking the stolen chat session is containment, not the erasure of a program from a disk. A green dot is not identity.\n',
    },
    'Image-based attacks': {
        'body': '\nSome attacks are not a picture of a fake page. They are bytes inside an image file that a viewer, a browser, or a thumbnail helper mishandles. The person thinks a ticket simply has a photo attached. The program that decodes the file is parsing a crafted structure: a length field, a color table, or a compressed segment the coder did not expect. If that parser fails in a dangerous way, it can crash or, worse, run the attacker\'s instructions while the file still looks like a picture. Nobody has to turn on a macro. Building the preview can be enough.\n\nBrowsers, mail panes, document tools, and help-desk consoles all link image libraries, and a bug in the library is inherited by every program that uses it. The fix belongs on that library, not only on the icon a user clicks. This is a different mechanism from a spreadsheet that waits for someone to allow active content. Here the file format itself is the weapon, and the trusted viewer is the thing that mishandles it. A phone call has no decoder involved. A normal photograph does not kill the thumbnail process.\n\nOn Tuesday a ticket titled badge photo failed arrived with a file named error.jpg. The attachment was about 4 megabytes, far larger than a normal ID photo. Andre was the only person with admin rights on the jump host that morning, and he did not open the file there. He sent a copy to the detonation sandbox. The sandbox log showed the thumbnail process dying on a malformed JPEG marker before any picture was drawn. That crash is the clue to write down, along with the filename, the size, and the image-library version on the jump host. A genuine photo does not take the preview helper down.\n\nDefenders turn off preview panes on privileged machines, patch the image parsers, and keep untrusted pictures off jump hosts until a sandbox has opened them. Sandbox detonation is how you learn whether you are holding a parser attack or an ordinary photo of a broken badge printer. The attack is the malformed image. Refusing to open it on the admin desk is the defense. Calling that refusal the attack reverses the story students are supposed to tell. Write the crash and the library version in the ticket, and do not "just look" on the machine that holds domain admin while you are still unsure.\n\nA document that runs because someone allowed a macro is the file-based neighbor, even though both can arrive as attachments. Students also mix this with a screenshot used only as bait, where the pixels show a fake login and the file format is harmless. If the harm is a person typing a password they see in the picture, you are looking at a lure carried by an image. If the harm is the viewer mishandling the file structure, you are looking at this attack. The separator is whether the bytes, not the scene in the photo, are what the program mishandles.\n',
        'r0': '\nAn image attack of this kind abuses the parser, not the scene in the photo. A length field, a color table, or a compressed segment is crafted so a viewer or a thumbnail helper mishandles it. In the clinic ticket the file was named error.jpg and was about 4 megabytes, and the sandbox thumbnail process died on a malformed JPEG marker before drawing pixels. No macro prompt was required. Preview panes on a jump host are enough to reach the bug. Patch the shared image library, because every program that links it inherits the flaw. Record the library version next to the crash.\n',
        'r1': '\nA picture file can be more than a picture. The viewer can mishandle the file itself. A preview pane can be enough to trigger it. Nobody has to allow a macro. Browsers and mail tools share picture libraries. A crash in the preview helper is a clue. Do not open that file on an admin machine. Send a copy to a safe sandbox first. Write down the file name and the size. Patch the library the viewer uses. A spreadsheet macro is a different attack. A phone call is a different attack.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic file room accepts folders of patient photos from the front desk. Most folders hold ordinary prints. One folder contains a print whose paper is the right size but whose layered backing hides a spring that snaps when a clerk runs it through the scanner. The picture on the front can be a normal face. The harm is in the object the machine tries to read, not in the face. Staff are told to keep unknown folders off the scanner that sits beside the records terminal and to use the small isolated scanner instead. The unknown photo works the same way. The danger is the file the viewer tries to decode, not the scene a person expects to see.\n',
        'r4': '\nDo not open the attachment on a jump host or any machine that holds admin rights. Copy it to the sandbox and record whether the preview process crashes. Write down the filename, the size, and the image-library version on the privileged workstation. Turn preview panes off on those workstations and leave them off while you investigate. Patch the shared picture library after you note the version you found. Do not treat a thumbnail crash as a harmless broken photo.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call every attachment a document attack, or to call a harmless screenshot of a fake login this attack because it happens to be a picture. A macro that runs after someone allows content is the file neighbor. A voice demand has no image parser. This attack is the viewer or browser mishandling the file structure. Opening the file on the admin machine is not a defense. The crash of the thumbnail helper is the clue that separates a crafted image from an ordinary photo.\n',
    },
    'File-based attacks': {
        'body': "\nDocuments and archives are how work moves between a clinic and its partners, so people open them with little hesitation. That habit is the path. The attack rides in a spreadsheet, a document, a compressed archive, or a disk-image file the recipient already knows how to open. The dangerous step is often a prompt that asks for macros or other active content, after which a payload starts. Sometimes a nested archive or a double extension hides a script behind a familiar invoice name. The person believes they are reading totals. The office program is running attacker instructions because the file was built to do that once content is allowed.\n\nAn image whose decoder crashes on a bad header is a different case, because nobody is asked to enable anything. Here the bait is a document or an archive people are used to trusting. A voice call has no file to hash. A partner name makes the bait believable: a real invoice from that supplier arrived last week, and this sheet looks like the next one in the series. The mail channel may have carried it, but this label belongs to the file itself, not to the mailbox that happened to deliver it.\n\nHelen in accounts received a macro-enabled spreadsheet inside a thread she keeps with a supplier. The supplier mailbox had been misused, so the thread looked continuous. Protected View showed a bar asking her to enable content so the totals would calculate. She left the bar alone. A security copy, opened in a sandbox with content allowed only there, recorded a child process started by the office application within a second. That process called an address the supplier's normal sheets never use. The child process, the file hash, and the sender belong together in the ticket.\n\nDefenders block macros that arrive from the internet zone, leave Protected View on, and detonate unknown partner files before an accountant enables content. Quarantine the message so a second desk does not open the same sheet. Blocking macros is a defense. The attack is the document that runs a payload once content is allowed. Training people never to enable content just to see totals supports that defense, and it does not turn the malicious file into a safety feature. Collect the hash before anyone deletes the only copy you have.\n\nStudents see an attachment and jump to the image label, or they see a partner name and talk only about the mailbox. The channel might be mail, chat, or a download link. This label is the document or archive. If a thumbnail library crashes on a JPEG and no macro is involved, that is the image case. If someone is pushed to read a password aloud, there is no file to hash. The separator is a document or archive people are accustomed to opening, which then runs something they did not sit down to run.\n",
        'r0': "\nA file-based attack uses a document or archive the reader expects to open. Macro-enabled spreadsheets are the common form: the payload waits until content is allowed, then the office process can start a child process. In Helen's case the sandbox showed that child within a second of enabling content, calling an address her supplier's normal workbooks never use. Protected View and an internet-zone macro block keep the payload from running on her desk. Record the file hash, the sender, and the process tree. An image-parser crash with no macro prompt is a different class of attachment, even when both arrived in mail.\n",
        'r1': '\nA familiar document can carry the attack. Spreadsheets and archives are common bait. The file may ask you to enable content. The payload runs after that permission. A partner name makes the sheet look normal. Do not enable content to see totals. Open a copy in a safe sandbox. Write down the file hash and the sender. Block macros that come from outside. A broken picture preview is different. A phone demand has no file. The attack is the file that runs code. Quarantine it so another desk cannot open it.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic kitchen receives ingredient boxes from the same dairy every week. Staff slice them open because the label matches last week's delivery. One box has the right logo and a packing slip, and under the slip sits a jar whose lid is wired to start a timer when it is twisted. Nothing looks odd until someone follows the usual step of opening the lid to check the date. The rule is to leave an unexpected inner package closed and to let a clerk inspect it on a side counter, away from the ovens. The familiar box is why the jar almost gets opened. A partner spreadsheet works the same way. The dangerous part starts when someone allows the extra content the file asks for.\n",
        'r4': "\nLeave Protected View on and do not enable content on the accountant's machine. Detonate a copy in the sandbox and record any child process and the address it calls. Write the file hash, the sender, and the message time in the ticket. Quarantine the original so another desk cannot open it. Keep the internet-zone macro block in place while you investigate. Do not delete the only copy before the hash is stored.\n",
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to label every attachment an image attack, or to name only the mailbox and never the file. A parser crash in a picture viewer, with no macro, is the image neighbor. A spoken password demand has no file hash. This attack is a document or archive people are used to opening. Enabling content is what the attacker wants. Blocking macros and using a sandbox are defenses, not other names for the attack.\n',
    },
    'Voice-call attacks': {
        'body': '\nA ringing phone creates pressure a mailbox item does not. The caller can sound senior, hurried, and slightly annoyed, and that performance pushes the listener to skip the check that matters: hang up and call back on a number the organization already published. Caller ID can be spoofed, so the screen may show the main switchboard while the voice belongs to a stranger. The path is the live call. The attack is the demand made on that call, usually a secret read aloud, a code that just appeared, or a payment approved before the bank closes.\n\nNothing in a voice proves a role. Callers claim to be a new finance lead, an auditor in the lobby, or a board member who cannot wait. A callback on a known number, or a walk to the real desk, is the defense. Doing what the voice asks is the attack succeeding. Answers that describe the callback as if it were the attack have the direction backward. A patched desk phone does not stop the push, because the weakness is a skipped callback, not a missing handset update.\n\nAt 4:40 the clinic front desk took a call whose caller ID matched the main published number. The voice said he was the new finance lead, that a vendor wire was failing, and that Dana must read him the shared portal password before the bank closed. She did not read it. She wrote the time, the claimed name, and the caller-ID digits, hung up, and dialed the finance director on the extension printed on the internal card. He was in a meeting and had placed no such call. The spoofed number and the refused password are the ticket facts.\n\nDefenders publish a callback rule for any request for a password, a code, or a payment, and they support the person who refuses a loud caller. If the desk system keeps a recording, store it with the ticket rather than playing it around the office. Do not coach people to argue with the caller or to offer a partial secret. Spoofed caller ID is a mechanism of the attack, not a side detail. Urgency is the pressure. The defense is the hang-up and the second call on a number that was already known before the phone rang.\n\nA package text is a different path even when the same criminal calls a minute later. An image parser and a macro file are not on the line. Students who hear the word password and answer only with the mailbox label miss the artifact: a live call, a spoofed number, and a demand to speak a secret. The technique name for a voice phish applies when the question asks what kind of lie it was. When the question asks which channel carried the demand, the answer is the call. The separator is the voice, and the callback that should have happened before any secret was spoken.\n',
        'r0': "\nCaller ID can display the clinic main number, or a director mobile, while the voice is someone else. That spoof is enough to make a desk skip the callback. In Dana's ticket the call arrived at 4:40, the ID matched the published main number, and the demand was the shared vendor-portal password read aloud. The defense is to hang up and dial an extension already printed on an internal card, not a number the caller provides. A recording, if the phone system retains one, is evidence. Use the call itself when the stem asks for the path, and the voice-phish name when it asks what kind of lie was told.\n",
        'r1': '\nA phone call can push people to skip a check. The voice may sound senior and rushed. The number on the screen can be faked. Do not read a password aloud. Do not read a fresh code aloud. Hang up before you argue. Call back on a number you already had. A loud tone is not identity. Write down the time and the claimed name. A text message is a different path. A picture file is a different path. The attack is the demand on the call. A patched phone does not stop this pressure.\n',
        'r2': "\nThe clinic front door has a speaker that visitors use when the desk is busy. Most voices are patients who are actually standing outside. One afternoon a voice uses the director's first name and tells the desk to buzz open the medication room because an inspection is starting. Nobody can see a face. The right habit is to leave the door locked and to reach the director on the inside extension posted above the phone. A familiar tone through a speaker is not a badge. A work call works the same way. Until someone calls back on a number the clinic already published, the voice on the line is not proof of who is speaking.\n",
        'r4': '\nWrite down the time, the claimed role, and the caller-ID digits before you hang up. Do not read a password, a code, or an account number on that call. Call back using the extension printed on the internal card, not a number the caller just gave you. Ask the real person whether they placed the call and record the answer. Keep any desk recording with the ticket. Do not change a payment or a password until that callback is finished.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to name only the mailbox because a password was involved, or to describe the callback as if the callback were the attack. The attack is the demand spoken on the call. Hanging up and dialing a known number is the defense. A text that arrives around the same time is a separate path. Caller ID matching the main number does not prove identity, because that field can be spoofed. Name the voice-phish technique only when the question asks for the kind of lie rather than the channel.\n',
    },
    'Removable media': {
        'body': "\nA USB stick, a memory card, or an external disk can carry or start code when a work computer mounts it. That object is the path. People trust small drives because they use them for ordinary copies, so a stick found in a parking lot and labeled as something sensitive is built to be plugged in. When the operating system mounts the volume, a planted program, a shortcut, or an autorun-style action can run or drop a file. Some devices do not act as disks at all. They present themselves as a keyboard and begin typing as soon as they are inserted. Either way, the boundary is crossed by connecting the object to a work machine.\n\nAn old document reader is a different hole, and so is a management port left open for scanners. Here the opportunity appears because someone connected the media. A current laptop can still be hurt, because the attack uses a normal feature of removable storage or of a device that pretends to be a keyboard. The printed label is social bait. The code runs because the computer trusted the new device, not because a reader build was years out of date.\n\nJamal found a USB stick by the clinic dumpster. The sticker said salary adjustments Q2. He did not plug it into the nursing-station PC. He walked it to the security office. In the lab, an isolated machine mounted a copy and listed a shortcut in the root folder aimed at a program stored on the stick. Opening that folder on a normal workstation would have launched the program under Jamal's login. The sticker text, the shortcut target, and the fact that no work computer mounted the original are the ticket facts. The attack would have been the mount. Turning the stick in was the defense.\n\nDefenders tell staff to hand found media to security and never to mount it on a workstation, a laptop, or a jump host. Autorun is disabled, and device policy can limit storage to approved hardware. Those controls are defenses. They are not a description of the attack. The attack is code that runs or is planted when the drive is mounted, or keystrokes that start when a fake keyboard is inserted. Inspect the device only on a machine that holds no clinic data and no cached passwords. Write down the label, the find spot, and what the lab image showed. Checking one file on your own PC is still a mount.\n\nStudents blame an unpatched reader that chokes on a downloaded file, or a management service left open because no agent was installed. A file already sitting on an internal disk is not removable media. A reader bug is the client-software neighbor. An open remote-management listener is the agentless neighbor. The separator is a physical object inserted into the computer. If nothing was plugged in, you are in a different topic, even when a harmful file is part of the story.\n",
        'r0': "\nRemovable media becomes a path at mount time or at insertion. A found USB stick can carry a shortcut that launches a program from the volume when the folder opens, and some devices enumerate as a keyboard and type without a mount prompt the user understands. Autorun policy and device control are the brakes. Jamal's stick was labeled salary adjustments Q2 and was imaged only on an isolated lab machine. No nursing-station PC mounted the original. Record the finder, the location, the label, and the shortcut target before anyone is tempted to take a quick look on a work desktop.\n",
        'r1': '\nA found stick can harm a work computer. The danger starts when the computer mounts it. A label about pay is bait, not proof. Some devices pretend to be a keyboard. Turn found media in to security. Do not plug it into a workstation. Do not plug it into a laptop you use for mail. Checking one file on your own PC is still a mount. An old reader bug is a different problem. An open management service is a different problem. Write down where the stick was found. The attack is the connected device.\n',
        'r2': "\nA warehouse night shift finds a small case on the loading dock labeled payroll backup. Most cases on that dock are real deliveries with a matching slip. This one has no slip, only a tempting label. Unknown cases go to the cage, not onto a forklift and not into the office copier room. Opening it beside the office machines would trust a stranger's trunk inside the building. Staff can be curious and still leave it closed. Curiosity is not a reason to connect it. Found computer media works the same way. Plugging that case into a work machine is the moment a stranger gets a turn on the computer.\n",
        'r4': '\nDo not mount the device on a workstation, a laptop, or a jump host. Record the finder name, the place it was found, and the words on the label. Hand it to security for an isolated lab image. Write down any shortcut target or keyboard behavior the lab observes. Leave autorun disabled, and do not relax device rules while you look. Do not open a single file on your own PC to see whether the stick is empty.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to blame an old document reader, or an open management port, when the story starts with an object that was plugged in. A reader bug is the client-software neighbor. A scanner port with no resident agent is the agentless neighbor. This path is the physical media. Mounting it on a work computer gives the attacker the chance. That mount is not a harmless check you perform on your own desk. Handing the stick in, unmounted, is the defense.\n',
    },
    'Vulnerable client software': {
        'body': "\nThe program on the user's machine can be the hole. Browsers, document readers, and mail previewers sit where untrusted files and pages arrive, and an old build of that program is an attack path even when the operating system is current. The attacker sends a file or a page shaped for a bug that a newer build already refuses. The person does something ordinary, such as opening a statement, and the outdated client mishandles a font, a script engine, or an embedded object. Other applications on that host do not need to share this particular bug. The old reader is enough.\n\nA found USB stick is a different path, and so is a management listener left open for inventory scans. The same bytes can be harmless to a current reader and damaging to the build still installed on the desk. That difference is the point. If the current version would also have run attacker code only because the user allowed a macro, you are looking at a file-based trick more than at an unpatched client. If the current version would have refused the same bytes, the path is the old program.\n\nRuth's billing PCs still had document reader build 11.0.08. The vendor's fixed build was 11.0.23. A partner sent a one-page statement. On a test PC at 11.0.23, the reader showed a warning and refused to draw an embedded font. On a billing PC still at 11.0.08, the same file crashed the reader and spawned a helper process that the notes for 11.0.23 say that build no longer creates. The pair of version numbers belongs in the ticket next to the filename. The attack used the old client. Replacing the reader is the defense.\n\nDefenders inventory client versions, not only operating-system build numbers, and they patch browsers and readers on a short cycle because those programs parse hostile input all day. Remove extra plugins you cannot justify. Open unknown files in a current viewer, or in a sandbox, until the desk build matches the fixed release. Taking the old reader off the billing image removes this path. The attack is the booby-trapped file meeting a client that still contains the bug. A rebuild of an unrelated server does not touch the reader on Ruth's PC.\n\nStudents call every bad file a macro attack, even when no content was enabled and the only difference between a crash and a refusal was the reader version. They also blame removable media when nothing was plugged in, or they blame a missing management agent. The separator is an outdated program on the endpoint that parses a file or page a current build would have refused. A patched suite can still be talked into a macro, and that remains the file topic. Here the client build is the fact that separates a refusal from a compromise.\n",
        'r0': '\nClient software such as a browser or a document reader is the path when its build is behind the vendor fix. Billing PCs were on reader 11.0.08 while the fixed build was 11.0.23. The same statement was refused by 11.0.23 and crashed 11.0.08, which then spawned a helper the fixed notes say that build no longer creates. That version gap is the evidence. Patching the operating system does not replace the reader. Inventory the client version beside the host version, and treat a file the current build refuses as hostile to every older build still in the clinic. A macro prompt that a patched suite still honors is a separate file-based case, not this version gap.\n',
        'r1': '\nThe program on the desk can be the hole. Browsers and readers parse untrusted files. An old build may accept a file a new build refuses. Opening an ordinary statement can be enough. Compare the desk version with the current build. Patch the reader, not only the system. Extra plugins widen the hole. A found memory stick is a different path. An open management port is a different problem. A macro someone allows is a different story. Write down both version numbers. Retire the old reader from the billing PCs.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic eye lane keeps two chart scanners. The new scanner rejects a torn page that would jam the works. The old scanner, still in the corner because one doctor likes it, pulls that same page in and chews the feed. The page did not change. The machine did. Staff should retire the old scanner from the lane rather than keep feeding it questionable pages because it still powers on. Fixing the lane means replacing the weak machine, not closing the clinic. An outdated reader on a desk works the same way. The file a current program refuses is the file the old program may still try to open.\n',
        'r4': '\nRecord the client name and the exact build on the affected PC. Compare it with the vendor fixed build and write both numbers in the ticket. Do not open the file again on that PC. Patch or replace the reader, and confirm the new build is actually running. Check browsers and mail previewers on the same image, not only the operating system. Leave unused plugins disabled. Keep the old version number in the note even after the upgrade.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call this a macro attack when nobody enabled content, or to call it removable media when nothing was plugged in. The separator is the client version. A current build that refuses the file shows the bug is in the old build. Allowing a macro on an already patched suite is the file-based neighbor. An open scanner port is the agentless neighbor. Patching the reader is the defense. Replacing an unrelated server does not fix the program on the desk.\n',
    },
    'Agentless exposure': {
        'body': '\nSome management tools reach a host without leaving a permanent agent on it. They log in remotely, query the system, and disconnect. That design helps inventory, and it becomes an attack path when the remote interface is weak or reachable by more machines than the scanner. The listener exists because administrators chose not to install an agent. If the port is open to the whole network, anyone else on that network can attempt the same login the scanner uses. No malware has to be waiting on the disk. The attack is a connection to a management interface that was left broad and underprotected.\n\nWinRM on TCP 5985 is a common form. The clinic inventory team uses it so a central scanner can list software without an agent. On the file server, the host firewall allowed 5985 from the entire 10.0.0.0/8, not only from the scanner at 10.4.4.12. An intruder who already had a foothold on any clinic subnet could open that port and try passwords. The firewall rule and the listening service are the ticket facts. This is not a story about a missing antivirus product. It is the remote door that agentless scanning required, left wider than the one host that needed it.\n\nA USB stick is a different path, and an old reader that crashes on a font is a different path. Nothing was inserted, and the file-parsing client may be fully patched. The exposure is the network service used for administration. SNMP on UDP 161 with a published community string, or a remote shell whose key is copied too widely, follows the same pattern: no resident agent, and a control weaker than the access it grants. The service can be a legitimate tool and still be the path an attacker uses.\n\nDefenders restrict source addresses to the scanner and a jump host, require strong authentication, and log every management login. A separate management network is stronger than a rule that says the whole internal range may connect. Those steps are defenses. The attack is an intruder using the wide door. Narrowing the rule to the scanner contains the exposure. You do not have to install an agent that same hour for the story to make sense, and the inventory method is not itself malicious. Write the port, the allowed source range, and the scanner address before you change the rule.\n\nStudents hear "no agent" and decide the host lacks antivirus, or they picture a USB stick in a parking lot. Others see a listening port and stop at the generic open-port label. A database published on the public internet, with no tie to agentless administration, is that open-port neighbor. Here the port is open because scanners needed it, and the failure is that everyone else could reach it too. The separator is remote administration without a resident agent, plus a remote interface that is wider or weaker than the job requires.\n',
        'r0': '\nAgentless management logs in, collects data, and leaves no permanent agent behind. The clinic scanner uses WinRM on TCP 5985. The file server allowed that port from 10.0.0.0/8 instead of only from the scanner at 10.4.4.12, so any internal host could attempt the login. Restricting the source to the scanner and a jump host removes the extra exposure. SNMP on UDP 161 with a published community string is the same class of mistake when it is reachable beyond the management hosts. Record the port, the allow-rule, and the scanner address before editing the firewall. Installing an antivirus agent would not, by itself, close a WinRM listener that the whole 10.0.0.0/8 can already reach.\n',
        'r1': '\nManagement can reach a host without a resident agent. The remote door exists so a scanner can log in. If everyone inside can reach it, an intruder can too. Use a strong login on that door. Allow only the scanner and a jump host. A found stick is a different path. An outdated reader is a different problem. Missing antivirus is a different complaint. Write down who may connect before you change it. Do not leave that door open to everyone. The attack is use of the wide management door.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic keeps a side door for the linen vendor. The vendor has no desk inside, so nobody stands there all day. The door is supposed to open only when that vendor's truck is in the alley. Someone props it with a brick so any passing delivery can enter. After that, it is no longer a vendor door. Anyone in the alley can try the handle. Staff should note who is allowed through before they change the lock, and they should not brick it open for convenience. Agentless management works the same way. A remote entrance left open for a scanner is also open to anyone else who can reach that entrance.\n",
        'r4': '\nRecord the listening port and the source range on the firewall before you change either one. Identify the scanner address and confirm the inventory team still needs the access. After the note is in the ticket, limit the rule to that scanner and a jump host. Require a strong login and keep the management log. Do not install random software only so you can say an agent exists. Do not call the scanner itself the attack.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to answer missing antivirus, or a found memory stick, when the stem describes a scanner port left open to the whole internal network. An old reader is the client-software neighbor. Removable media needs something plugged in. A forgotten database on the public internet is the open-port neighbor when agentless administration is not the story. Here the listener exists so a scanner can work without an agent, and the failure is that everyone else could use it. Narrowing the source list is the defense.\n',
    },
    'Unsupported systems': {
        'body': '\nAn unsupported system is one the vendor no longer fixes. When a new flaw is found in that platform, the hole stays open because no security patch will be issued for it. The machine can still boot, still serve files, and still sit on the internal network. What changed is the vendor\'s promise. After the support date, published bugs accumulate. Workarounds and network fences are all that remain, and they are compensations, not a return of vendor maintenance.\n\nThis is not the same as a supported server that is merely late on updates. In that case a fix exists and the failure is the delay. It is also not a zero-day on a product the vendor still maintains. A zero-day has no patch yet, but the vendor is still in the business of writing one. Unsupported means the stream of fixes has ended. Wireless reach and a lobby network jack are different paths. They describe how a person connects, not whether the platform will ever be repaired.\n\nThe clinic imaging archive still runs a server edition whose support ended last March. It lives at 10.8.20.15 and accepts file shares from the reading-room PCs. Last month a local privilege bug was published for that platform. The current edition received a patch. The advisory for this edition said no fix would be issued. The ticket should show the support-end date, the address 10.8.20.15, and the advisory line that no patch is planned. Anyone who can log on locally inherits a bug the vendor has declined to close.\n\nDefenders isolate the host, allow only the reading-room PCs that still depend on it, and schedule a replacement. Isolation is containment of the exposure. There is no eradication step that consists of installing a patch, because the patch will not exist. After the archive is migrated, powering the old server off removes the platform. Until that day, a firewall rule is a defense, not a claim that the system is supported again. Write the end-of-support date before you argue about the risk, so the ticket is anchored to a vendor fact rather than to a feeling that the server seems fine.\n\nStudents call every missing patch an unsupported system. If the vendor still offers the fix and the clinic has not installed it, the system is behind, not unsupported. Students also swap this label with a guest wireless network or a lobby jack because all three feel like "old and loose." Radio reach and a physical port are connection paths. A zero-day on this week\'s supported build is the opposite time problem: the fix has not been written yet, rather than a product line the maker already closed. The separator is the vendor\'s decision to stop issuing fixes.\n',
        'r0': '\nUnsupported means the vendor has ended security fixes for that platform, so newly published flaws stay open. The clinic imaging server at 10.8.20.15 ran an edition whose support ended last March. A local privilege bug patched on the current edition was marked no fix planned for this one. That advisory line is the evidence. A supported host that is simply late on patches is a different failure, because the package exists. Isolating 10.8.20.15 to the reading-room PCs is containment. Replacement, not a waiting patch cycle, is how the platform leaves the network. A guest wireless name and a lobby jack do not answer the support question this address raises.\n',
        'r1': '\nThe maker no longer sends fixes for this system. New flaws stay open on purpose of that choice. The computer can still be on the network. A late patch on a supported system is different. A flaw with no fix yet, on a living product, is different. Wireless reach is a different problem. A lobby wall jack is a different problem. Fence the old system until you can replace it. Write down the date support ended. Do not wait for a patch that will not come. Isolation buys time. It does not restore support.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic owns a boiler the manufacturer stopped making parts for last spring. The boiler still heats water. When a safety valve design is recalled, the current model gets a new valve and this one gets a letter saying no part will be shipped. Staff can rope off the room and check the gauge more often. They cannot order the recall part. Leaving it in service because the rooms are warm confuses function with support. An unfixed computer platform works the same way. Once the maker has ended fixes, every new flaw remains, and a fence is not a renewed maintenance contract.\n',
        'r4': '\nFind the vendor support-end date and write it in the ticket. Record the host address and which users or PCs still reach it. Do not assume a patch will appear if the advisory says none is planned. Limit network access to the systems that still depend on it, and note that limit as containment. Plan the replacement separately from the firewall change. Do not mark the system supported because it still boots.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call a supported server unsupported merely because the clinic is late installing a patch that exists. The trap also runs the other way: a flaw with no patch yet, on a product the vendor still maintains, is not this label. Wireless and wired access are connection paths, not a support status. Isolation contains the exposure and does not bring patches back. The separator is a vendor that has stopped issuing fixes for that platform.\n',
    },
    'Wireless networks': {
        'body': '\nA wireless network can be joined from outside the building, which is why a weak shared secret or an open guest path matters. The radio does not stop at the wall. Anyone in the parking lot who can complete an association is on that network, or on whatever that network was bridged to. WPA2-Personal uses one passphrase for every client. If the phrase is short, an attacker who captures the handshake can test guesses offline without staying connected. WPA3 is built to resist that offline guessing. An open guest name with no client isolation lets one associated phone talk to other clients.\n\nThe clinic still advertised an old guest name, ClinicGuest, with no client isolation. It was bridged onto VLAN 20, the same VLAN as the front-desk printer. A phone associated from the parking lot and opened the printer administration page on TCP 80. No clinic account was required. Separately, the staff name used WPA2-Personal with an eight-character passphrase made from the clinic\'s own name. The ticket facts are the guest name, the missing isolation, VLAN 20, the printer page reached from a non-staff device, and the short staff passphrase. The attack is the association from outside and the reach it produced. A long passphrase and a fenced guest network are defenses.\n\nWired jacks are the neighboring path, and they need a cable. An unsupported server is about a vendor who stopped sending fixes, not about radio. A guest network that isolates clients and cannot route to printers is a different design from the one in this ticket. Leaving a legacy protection mode such as WEP enabled on the same radios creates yet another easy association, and that mode is retired for a reason. The attack is not "having wireless." The attack is joining a weakly built wireless network from a place the clinic does not control, then touching something that should have stayed inside.\n\nDefenders move staff devices to WPA3 or to WPA2 with a long unique passphrase, turn on client isolation for guests, and put guests on a VLAN that cannot reach printers, file shares, or cameras. They disable retired protection modes. Those changes are the defense. Capturing a handshake against a short shared secret, or loading an admin page from the parking lot, is the attack. Write the SSID, the protection mode, the VLAN, and what a test phone could open before you rotate the passphrase, so the ticket shows the exposure rather than only the new secret.\n\nStudents treat every network question as a cable problem, or they call the guest path unsupported because it feels old. Unsupported is a vendor support status. A visitor who plugs into a lobby jack never used the radio. If the stem shows a parking-lot association, a shared passphrase, or a guest client reaching an internal admin page, the path is wireless. The separator is reach without a jack, and the quality of the protection mode and the isolation that were supposed to limit that reach.\n',
        'r0': '\nWireless association does not require a jack, so the parking lot is in range. WPA2-Personal uses a shared passphrase. An eight-character clinic-name passphrase can be tested offline after the handshake is captured. WPA3 is the mode that resists that offline guessing. The old guest name ClinicGuest had no client isolation and sat on VLAN 20 with the front-desk printer, so a phone from the lot opened the printer admin page on TCP 80. Put guests on a VLAN that cannot reach internal hosts, and retire WEP if any radio still offers it. A lobby cable would be a different path, because this exposure started with an association and no jack.\n',
        'r1': '\nPeople can join a wireless network from the parking lot. A short shared password can be guessed later. A guest network can see other machines if isolation is off. A printer page should not open from that guest. Put guests on their own separate network. Use a long secret for staff. Turn off old weak protection modes. A wall jack is a different path. A system with no vendor fixes is a different problem. Write down the network name and what a test phone could reach. The attack is the outside join.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic courtyard has a gate that was meant for patients, with a word painted on the gate that everyone in town already knows. The gate opens onto the hallway where the copier sits, not onto a bench outside. Strangers do not need a key from the front desk. They need the painted word and a walk across the courtyard. Staff should give the courtyard its own bench area that cannot open the hallway, and they should change a word the town already repeats. Radio access works the same way. Joining from outside the walls is the attack, and a fence between guests and the copier is the defense.\n',
        'r4': '\nWrite down each wireless name, its protection mode, and whether guest clients are isolated. From a phone that is not a staff device, record what internal pages you can open, then stop. Do not rotate the staff secret until that reach is written in the ticket. Move guests off any network that also holds printers or file shares. Disable retired protection modes and leave them disabled. Note the VLAN or equivalent separation you found before you change it.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call a parking-lot association a wired-jack problem, or to call a weak guest network unsupported because it seems old. Unsupported means the vendor ended fixes. Wired access needs a cable in a jack. This path is radio reach. A short shared secret and a guest client that can open an internal admin page are attacks against a weak design. Isolation and a long secret are defenses. Having wireless service at all is not the weakness the question is naming.\n',
    },
    'Wired networks': {
        'body': '\nA wired path is a physical jack that places a stranger on an inside network. Lobbies, conference rooms, and unused offices often still have live wall ports. If that port is an access port on the staff VLAN, a visitor who plugs in receives a staff address from DHCP and can talk to internal hosts. No radio and no stolen password are required. The cable is the path. The attack is the visitor landing inside, not the existence of Ethernet in the building.\n\nThe control that belongs here is authentication at the port. With 802.1X, the switch does not grant the staff VLAN until the computer presents a credential or a certificate. Unauthenticated jacks should land on a guest VLAN that cannot reach charts, printers\' admin pages, or file servers. Sticky learning of a MAC address is a weak substitute, because MAC addresses are easy to copy and an empty port will learn the first machine that appears. Disabling unused jacks removes the path entirely.\n\nA visitor sat in the clinic lobby and plugged a laptop into the jack under the brochure table. The laptop received 10.8.0.44, which is on the staff subnet, and a gateway of 10.8.0.1. No 802.1X prompt appeared. From that address the laptop resolved internal names. The switch showed interface Gi1/0/14 as an access port on VLAN 10 with no authentication. Those four facts, the address, the gateway, the interface, and the missing challenge, belong in the ticket. The attack was the plug-in. The staff address was the proof it worked.\n\nDefenders enable 802.1X on live jacks, move open lobby ports to a guest VLAN, and shut ports that have no business reason to be up. Those are defenses. The attack is a cable turning a visitor into an inside host. Changing the VLAN after you have recorded Gi1/0/14 and the address 10.8.0.44 keeps the evidence intact. Do not describe the jack as a monitoring feature. It was an entrance. A wireless test from the parking lot would not have used this port, so do not file the lobby incident as a radio problem.\n\nStudents mix this with wireless because both are "network access," and they mix it with unsupported systems because an old switch feels related. Wireless needs no jack. Unsupported is a vendor decision about patches. A port that challenges for 802.1X and fails closed onto a dead VLAN is not this exposure. The separator is a cable in a wall port that hands out an inside address. If the stem includes a radio name or a passphrase, you are on the wireless topic instead.\n',
        'r0': '\nA live wall jack is an attack path when it drops the visitor onto the staff network. In the lobby, interface Gi1/0/14 was an access port on VLAN 10 with no 802.1X challenge. The visitor laptop received 10.8.0.44 and gateway 10.8.0.1, both staff-subnet values, and could resolve internal names. 802.1X should hold the staff VLAN until a credential or certificate succeeds. Unauthenticated jacks belong on a guest VLAN. Sticky MAC learning is weak because the address can be copied. Record the interface, VLAN, and leased address before you shut the port. A parking-lot association would not have produced Gi1/0/14, so do not file the lobby evidence as a radio incident.\n',
        'r1': '\nA wall jack can put a visitor on the inside network. A cable is enough. No radio is required. The laptop may get a staff address automatically. The switch should demand a login before that happens. Unused jacks should be turned off. A guest jack must not reach staff systems. Copying a hardware address is a weak lock. A parking lot radio join is a different path. A maker who stopped sending fixes is a different problem. Write down the jack and the address it gave out. The attack is the plug-in.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic lobby has a side cupboard with a power socket that was meant for a lamp. Someone later tied that socket to the same circuit as the record room. A visitor who brings a plug can feed equipment straight onto that circuit. The cupboard door was never a lock, only a door. Staff should move unknown sockets onto a hallway circuit that cannot reach the record room, and they should cap sockets nobody uses. A network jack works the same way. A cable in an unauthenticated lobby jack can place a stranger on the staff side of the building, and the fix is to stop handing out that inside connection.\n',
        'r4': '\nFind the switch interface, the VLAN, and whether the port asks for a credential. Plug in only a test machine you control, write down the address it receives, and unplug it. Do not browse internal systems from that test beyond the name resolution you need as proof. Disable unused lobby jacks or move them to a guest network after the notes are saved. Do not rely on a learned hardware address as the only control. Leave the original interface name in the ticket.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to answer wireless when the visitor used a cable, or to answer unsupported because the switch is old. Wireless is the radio path and needs no jack. Unsupported means fixes have ended. This attack is a wall port that grants an inside address without authentication. A port that demands a credential and fails closed is not the same exposure. Sticky learning of a hardware address is a weak defense, not proof the jack was safe.\n',
    },
    'Bluetooth': {
        'body': 'A conference room is small enough that a radio meant for a headset can reach the next chair. Bluetooth is a short-range radio path. When a laptop stays open to new pairings, a phone beside it can request a bond or probe the adapter. The radio sits in the 2.4 gigahertz band, and Class 2 reaches about ten meters. That distance is the room itself, which is plenty for a person who sits down next to a machine. No software bug has to be present. The adapter is following an open pairing mode.\n\nHow the bond is confirmed decides how easy the abuse is. Just Works finishes with no number the owner can compare, so a stranger can complete a bond while the owner watches a slide. A stronger check shows the same six-digit code on both screens and continues only when a person accepts the match. After a keyboard is bonded, it can inject keystrokes. After a headset is bonded, the microphone can stay live once the meeting is over. None of this joins the office wireless network, because there is no network name and no office subnet. It is also not a database answering on a public address. The two devices only need to be near each other.\n\nConference room B held the borrowed laptop FRONT-DESK-4 with pairing left on from 8:00 until 16:10. At 10:42 the adapter log recorded a pair request from a phone named Pixel-guest. That phone was not on the clinic inventory. The mode in the log was Just Works, so the screen never showed a six-digit code. The laptop stayed locked, and no bond was stored. Mina still treated the request as the incident. She copied the room, the laptop name, the foreign phone name, the clock time, and the pairing mode into the ticket before she changed the adapter.\n\nPeople who defend this path leave pairing off except during the minute a known device is added. Headsets and keyboards should require the code shown on both screens. Bonds created in a public room should be removed the same day. Desktops with no need for this radio should have it disabled by policy. Those controls are the defense. The attack is the nearby pair or probe, and any bond that lets a stranger type or listen. A firewall at the building edge never sees this radio, so a firewall change does not close the path. The log line goes into the ticket first. Clearing the adapter history before that copy destroys the evidence.\n\nExam questions often rename the next-chair request as a wireless network join, or as an open service port, because both ideas sound like something that answers. A camera that still accepts the password printed on its sticker is the shipped login, even when that camera also contains a radio. The separating fact is physical nearness together with a pair or probe on this radio. A fully patched laptop remains exposed if a profile left pairing on all day. Being inside the same room is already close enough.\n',
        'r0': 'Class 2 radio in the 2.4 gigahertz band reaches about ten meters, a conference chair rather than a campus. Just Works completes a bond with no six-digit code for the owner to compare. Numeric comparison shows that code on both screens and waits for a person to accept it. Room B left the borrowed laptop FRONT-DESK-4 open to pairing from 8:00 to 16:10. At 10:42 the phone Pixel-guest, missing from inventory, sent a pair request. No bond was stored because the screen was locked. A stored keyboard bond could inject keystrokes afterward, and a headset bond could keep a microphone live. Policy should open pairing only while a known device is added. This path is not an office wireless join and not a database listener. Record the adapter line before anyone erases it.\n',
        'r1': 'A nearby radio can still be a trap. A laptop may accept a new pair. A phone in the next chair is close enough. Some pairs finish with no code to compare. Ask for a matching code on both screens. A bonded keyboard can type for a stranger. A bonded headset can keep listening. This is not the office wireless network. Turn pairing off when the known device is added. Remove bonds made in a public room. A patched laptop can still accept a pair. Write down the phone name and the time. The unwanted pair is the attack.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic waiting room keeps a small intercom for a parent who steps outside with a child. Most of the day the switch is off. One afternoon a clerk leaves it on, and the person in the next chair can press talk and be heard at the nurses station. Nobody checks who holds the other handset. The plastic looks harmless because families use it every week. Staff should turn it off when the known parent is done. Curiosity is not a reason to leave the switch open all day. A work laptop that keeps accepting new pairings behaves the same way. A phone in the next chair can request a bond, and that request is the attack a defender should record before the radio is cleared.\n',
        'r4': 'Look at the adapter log before you change any setting. Write down the room, the laptop name, the foreign device name, the time, and whether a bond was stored. Do not clear that history until those facts are in the ticket. After the note exists, leave pairing off except while you add a known device. Require a code on both screens for a headset or a keyboard. Do not call a firewall change the fix for this radio.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call a next-chair pair request a wireless network join, or an open port on a server. A sticker password is the shipped login neighbor. The attack is the nearby pair or probe. Requiring a code on both screens, and turning pairing off, are defenses. A building firewall does not see this radio. A patched laptop can still accept a pair if that mode was left on. Nearness in the same room is enough. An open pairing mode is what the attacker uses.\n',
    },
    'Open service ports': {
        'body': '\nAn open service port is a listening door. Some process has bound a port and will answer clients that can route to it. If that service is forgotten, buggy, or facing a network it should never see, the port is the attack path. Attackers scan addresses, notice the answer, read the banner, and try whatever flaw or login the service exposes. The clinic did not have to be targeted by name. A wide scan is enough once the door answers. Availability of a needed service on a private network is ordinary operations. A forgotten listener on a public address is an attack path.\n\nAn external scan of the clinic public address 203.0.113.20 showed TCP 3306 open. The banner named a database build that operations did not remember publishing. A lab change the year before had forwarded the port, and the rule outlived the lab. No VPN was required. From the internet the scanner received the banner in a single connection. The ticket facts are the public address, TCP 3306, the banner text, and the firewall rule that still allowed it. The attack is any party on the internet talking to that database. Closing the forward is the defense.\n\nDefault credentials are a neighboring problem and not the same fact. A port can be open while the password is unique, and a shipped password can exist on a camera whose admin port was supposed to be reachable only on a management network. Short-range radio pairing is a different path again. Agentless administration can also leave a listener, but the story there is a scanner port widened to the whole internal network. Here the stem is a service the clinic no longer meant to expose, answering on a public address. The listening socket is the path even before anyone guesses a password.\n\nDefenders scan from the outside, compare the results with the list of services that should be public, and remove the rest. Default deny on the firewall makes a forgotten forward obvious. Patching a database that remains reachable from the internet does not remove the path. It only changes which bugs are present. The attack is the connection to a service that should not have answered. Unpublishing TCP 3306 is the defense. Write the address, the port, and the banner before you delete the rule, so the ticket still proves what the internet could see that morning.\n\nStudents see a password printed on a device and name only default credentials, even when the question\'s fact is a port that answers from the public internet. They also call every listening port a wireless issue because both involve "access." Radio association is not a TCP banner. If the evidence is a scan line showing a host and a port, with a service the owner did not intend to publish, this is the label. The separator is the listening door itself, not the quality of the password behind it and not the radio that was never used.\n',
        'r0': '\nA listening port is a path when the service is forgotten, flawed, or reachable from the wrong network. An external scan of 203.0.113.20 found TCP 3306 open, and the banner named a database build. The forward was a leftover lab rule, so the internet could speak to the database with no VPN. Record the address, the port, and the banner before removing the rule. A unique password would not make that public listener appropriate. Default deny at the firewall is the control that keeps the next leftover from answering. Patching alone leaves the door in place. A sticker password on a camera is a different fact from a database banner on a public address.\n',
        'r1': '\nIf a service answers, strangers can knock. A forgotten database on a public address is a door. Scanners find it by trying many addresses. The banner tells them what is running. A needed service on a private network is ordinary. A leftover public forward is not. Closing the forward is the defense. A sticker password is a different problem. A radio network is a different problem. Write down the address and the port before you close it. The attack is the unwanted answer. Patching does not remove a door you left open.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic loading dock has a small service hatch that used to accept supply samples. The program ended, but the hatch still opens when anyone pushes it. Delivery drivers and passersby can stick a hand through and touch the shelf inside. The hatch is not a sign of a healthy clinic. It is a leftover opening. Staff should list every hatch that is supposed to work, close the others, and write down what they found before they paint it shut. A listening network service works the same way. An answer from a service the clinic no longer meant to expose is the attack path, and closing that answer is the defense.\n',
        'r4': '\nScan the public addresses from outside and list every port that answers. Write the address, the port, and the banner into the ticket before you change the firewall. Compare the list with the services that are supposed to be public. Remove leftover forwards after that note exists. Do not treat a patch as a substitute for closing an unintended listener. Leave the original rule text in the ticket so the exposure stays documented.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to name a sticker password when the evidence is a public port that answers, or to name wireless because the word access appeared. Default credentials are the published login. Radio association is a different path. This attack is the listening service, especially one the owner did not mean to expose. A strong password does not make a forgotten public database appropriate. Closing the listener is the defense. The banner and the port are the facts that separate this from a password-only story.\n',
    },
    'Default credentials': {
        'body': "\nDefault credentials are the username and password a vendor ships with a product. They are printed in manuals, on stickers, and in support articles, so they are not a secret. Anyone who can reach the sign-in page and who has read the public documentation can try them. The attack is that login. Leaving the shipped pair in place after the device is installed is what makes the path real. A unique password you chose and stored in a vault is not this topic, even if you later handle it badly in some other way.\n\nCameras, printers, switches, and small appliances are the usual places this shows up, because they arrive ready to work and the installer moves on. The clinic received a hallway camera last week. The web page on that camera still accepted the user admin and the six-character password printed on the bottom sticker. The installation ticket had been closed with a note that said mounted and aimed, and nobody had changed the login. From a staff laptop, the analyst opened the camera page, submitted the sticker pair, and landed in the admin view. The sticker text and the successful sign-in are the evidence. The attack is the use of a published secret. Changing the password before the camera is reachable is the defense.\n\nAn open port is the neighboring idea and answers a different question. A database listening on a public address is a door, whether or not the password is the vendor default. A camera admin page that was meant to exist on the camera VLAN, but still uses the manual password, is this topic. The door can be appropriate and the secret still be worthless. Short-range pairing with no password of this kind is a different radio problem. Do not collapse every login failure into one label.\n\nDefenders change shipped passwords before a device joins the network, then inventory gear that still answers to published pairs. Vendor lists of default logins are useful for that inventory because the attacker can read the same lists. Those steps are defenses. Guessing admin and the sticker string is the attack. Write down the device, the sticker value, and that the sign-in succeeded, then change the password and confirm the old pair fails. Do not call the sticker a temporary secret. It was never secret. A strong unique password on that same camera page would move the story out of this topic and back to ordinary administration.\n\nStudents see a listening port and stop there, even when the stem's decisive fact is a password printed by the vendor. They also treat any weak password a person chose, such as the clinic name, as a default. A human-chosen bad password is still a bad password, but it is not the pair the manual published. The separator is the shipped login that documentation already revealed. If the evidence is the sticker or the manual, this is the label, even when the port itself is one the camera is supposed to have.\n",
        'r0': "\nDefault credentials are the username and password printed by the vendor, often admin plus a short sticker string. They are documented, so they are not a secret. The new hallway camera still accepted admin and the six-character password on the bottom label after the install ticket was closed. A sign-in with that pair reached the admin view. Change the pair before the device is reachable, then confirm the sticker login fails. A public database port is a different fact: that topic is the listener, while this one is the published secret. Inventory cameras, printers, and switches against the vendor's published login list.\n",
        'r1': '\nThe password in the manual is not a secret. The password on the sticker is not a secret. Anyone who can open the page can try it. Cameras and printers often ship this way. Change the login before the device is reachable. A successful sticker login is the proof. Write down the device and the sticker text. Then make the old pair fail. A public listening service is a different problem. A password a person invented is not this shipped pair. The attack is using the published login.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic supply closet arrives with a padlock that uses the combination printed on the packing tag. Every buyer of that closet gets the same tag. Leaving the tag on the hasp means any person in the hallway can read it and open the door. The closet can be in the right room and still be trivial to open. Staff should set a new combination before the closet is placed in a hallway, and they should throw the tag away. A shipped device login works the same way. The attack is reading the published combination, and choosing a new one before anyone else can reach the door is the defense.\n',
        'r4': '\nFind devices that still accept the vendor username and the sticker or manual password. Write down the device name, where it sits, and the published pair you tried. Do not leave that pair working while you finish the paperwork. Change it, then confirm the old sign-in fails, and store the new secret in the vault. Do not invent a second shared password and tape it to the device. Check printers and switches from the same shipment, not only the camera that started the ticket.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to name only an open port when the decisive fact is a vendor password printed on a sticker or in a manual. The listener and the published secret are different. A password an employee chose, even a weak one, is not the shipped pair. The attack is signing in with the documented login. Changing it before the device is reachable is the defense. A camera page that should exist can still be this weakness if the sticker password remains.\n',
    },
    'Managed service providers in the supply chain': {
        'body': "\nA managed service provider that holds administrative rights inside your network is a high-value attack path. The provider's job is to reach many customers' machines, often with one remote tool and powerful accounts. When that tool or those accounts are compromised, the attacker does not start from the outside of each clinic. The attacker starts from a login that already works. One breach at the provider becomes a breach at every customer who shared that foothold. The path is the provider's access, not a phishing page the clinic built.\n\nThe clinic's outsourced desktop team uses one remote-support tool on 86 workstations. The tool's service account is a local administrator on each of those machines. There is no separate account per site, and the provider's staff share the password. A notice from the tool vendor said the provider tenant had been entered and sessions could be opened without a new prompt. The clinic saw a support session on the nursing-station PC at 02:14, when no ticket was open. That timestamp, the tool name, the count of 86 hosts, and the shared local-admin account are the ticket facts. The attack is the outsider riding the helper's keys. The helper relationship itself is not the attack.\n\nA vendor who ships a badge-printer update is a different role. That company delivers a product or a signed package. It does not sit all day with a remote shell on the nursing PCs. A supplier upstream of a software maker may alter a library before the maker even sees it, and that maker may never log into the clinic. Students merge all three because the word supply chain appears in each stem. The separator is live administrative reach into your network, concentrated through one provider that serves many customers.\n\nDefenders give the provider separate accounts per customer, remove shared domain or local admin where a narrower role will do, and require multi-factor authentication on those accounts. Session logs should be copied to a system the provider cannot erase. Alert on remote sessions that have no matching ticket. Those controls are the defense. The attack is use of the provider's foothold after the provider side is compromised, or abuse of access that was broader than the work required. Disabling the provider accounts and the remote tool's trust is containment. It is not the same step as deleting a virus from one PC after you have preserved evidence.\n\nIf the stem shows a company that remotely administers your endpoints, a shared tool, and a blast radius across customers, this is the label. A tainted library inside an application you installed is the software-provider story. A tampered badge in a shipment is a hardware story. Do not answer those when the evidence is a remote session at 02:14 from the desktop partner. Also do not describe the provider's contract as if the contract were the attack. The attack is the stolen or excessive administrative path.\n",
        'r0': '\nA managed service provider with admin rights is a path because one compromise reaches every customer of that foothold. The clinic tool was installed on 86 workstations, and its service account was a local administrator on each, with one shared password. A session appeared at 02:14 on a nursing PC with no open ticket after the tool tenant was reported entered. Separate accounts per customer, multi-factor authentication, and logs the provider cannot delete are the controls. Disabling the remote-tool trust is containment. A vendor who only ships a signed update does not have this standing access, and an upstream library change is a different stage of the chain.\n',
        'r1': '\nA helper with admin rights can be a path. One break there can reach many customers. A shared remote tool makes that worse. A shared password on that tool is worse still. A session with no ticket is a warning sign. Give each customer a separate login. Do not hand out broad admin for simple fixes. Keep logs the helper cannot erase. Turn off the remote trust if the helper is breached. A shipped product update is a different story. A part changed before the seller saw it is different too.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic hires a cleaning company and gives the crew one master key that opens every exam room and also the rooms of three other clinics on the contract. The crew is supposed to be there. The key is the problem when it is copied. A person who steals that key does not pick eighty locks. They walk in as the cleaners. The clinics should have given a key per building, kept a log the crew cannot rewrite, and pulled the master key the night the copying was discovered. A remote helper with broad access works the same way. The attack is the stolen key that already opens your rooms, not the fact that you hired help.\n',
        'r4': '\nList every remote tool the provider uses and the privilege of its account. Write down the host count and whether one password is shared across customers. Pull session logs and compare them with open tickets, including after-hours entries. Do not delete those logs while you investigate. Disable the provider account or the tool trust if the provider reports a compromise, and record that as containment. Do not leave a shared local-admin password in place after you have seen it used.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to lump every partner into one supply-chain answer. A company that ships a device or an update is the vendor. A firm that alters a component before that vendor receives it is the supplier. This label is the partner with live admin inside your network, often through one tool used for many customers. The attack is abuse of that access. Splitting accounts and requiring a second factor are defenses. A remote session with no ticket is evidence, not a normal feature of the contract.\n',
    },
    'Vendors in the supply chain': {
        'body': '\nA vendor in this sense is a company you buy from that ships software, devices, or updates into your environment. The delivery can carry a problem on purpose or by accident. A flawed firmware image, a trojaned installer, or an update pulled from the wrong server all ride the trust you already give that seller. The path is the delivery. The attack is the harmful or untrusted package entering because it appeared to come from the company you pay. Checking a signature before installation is the defense, not the definition of the attack.\n\nThe clinic\'s badge printer needed an update. The file arrived by mail from an address that looked like the vendor, and the tech was ready to run it on the print server. The published vendor signature did not match the file hash. A second copy downloaded from the vendor\'s own portal did match. The mismatched file is the incident. The ticket should hold both hashes, the mail sender, and the portal URL used for the good copy. Had the mismatched file been installed, the print server would have run whatever the sender had packaged. The attack is that installation of an altered delivery. Refusing the unsigned or wrongly signed file is how the tech stopped it.\n\nA managed provider who already has a remote tool on your PCs is a different path, because the access is standing administration rather than a shipment. A supplier upstream can tamper with a library before this vendor compiles the product, and the vendor may then sign the result in good faith. A valid signature proves the vendor\'s key signed the bytes. It does not, by itself, prove every upstream author was honest. In this topic the question is the company you buy from and the package they, or an impersonator of them, deliver to you.\n\nDefenders verify signatures or hashes against a value taken from the vendor\'s own channel, test updates before they reach the print server, and keep vendor remote access time-limited if the vendor must log in at all. They do not give a printer company a permanent domain admin account in order to install one driver. Those are defenses. The attack is the push of a bad package or a flawed device under the vendor\'s name. Quarantining the mismatched file preserves evidence. Installing it "just to see" on the print server is how the attack succeeds.\n\nStudents answer with the upstream supplier when the stem is a delivery the clinic received from the company on the purchase order. They answer with the managed provider when nobody has a standing remote shell. If the artifact is an update, a device, or an installer from the seller, and the question is whether that delivery can be trusted, this is the label. The separator is the company that ships to you, checked at the moment of delivery, rather than the earlier stage that fed that company or the partner who administers your desktops every night.\n',
        'r0': '\nA vendor path is a software, device, or update delivery from a company you buy from. The badge-printer file from lookalike mail failed the vendor signature check. The hash did not match the portal copy, which did verify. That pair of hashes is the evidence. A valid signature shows the vendor key signed those bytes. It does not prove an upstream author was honest, which is the supplier question instead. Standing remote admin by a desktop partner is the managed-provider path, not this shipment. Quarantine the mismatched file and install only the portal copy after the hashes are recorded. Standing admin on clinic PCs would be a different partner and a different path.\n',
        'r1': '\nThe company you buy from can deliver a bad update. The package may be altered or simply flawed. Check the signature before anyone installs it. A match means that seller signed those bytes. A mismatch means do not run the file. Keep the bad copy as evidence. A helper with everyday admin rights is a different path. A part changed further up the chain is different too. Write down both hashes. The attack is installing the untrusted delivery. Refusing it is the defense.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic kitchen receives a sauce tub from the distributor named on the weekly order. Most tubs are fine. One tub arrives with a seal that does not match the seal on the distributor's own photo, and the lot number is smudged. Cooking with it because the truck was the usual truck would trust the delivery instead of the seal. Staff set that tub aside and open a tub whose seal matches the photo. A software or device delivery works the same way. The attack is a bad package riding the seller's name, and checking the seal before use is the defense.\n",
        'r4': "\nDo not install the update on the print server while the signature or hash is unverified. Download a comparison copy from the vendor's own portal and write both hashes in the ticket. Save the rejected file so the mismatch can be rechecked. If the vendor must log in, make that access expire and do not grant standing domain admin for a driver install. Note the sender address of the delivery mail. Leave the production printer on the previous known-good build until the good copy is confirmed.\n",
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to name the upstream supplier, or the managed provider, for a package the clinic itself received from the seller. The supplier alters something before the vendor sees it. The managed provider holds standing admin. This attack is a harmful or untrusted delivery from the company you buy from. A failed signature check is the defense succeeding. Installing the file anyway would be the attack succeeding. A valid vendor signature still does not answer whether an earlier author in the chain was honest.\n',
    },
    'Suppliers in the supply chain': {
        'body': "\nA supplier, in this topic, sits upstream of the vendor you pay. The supplier builds a part, a library, or a subassembly that your vendor receives and then ships onward. Tampering or a flaw at that earlier stage can enter the product before your vendor inspects it. The vendor may compile, package, and even sign the result in good faith. Customers who trust only the vendor's logo never see the earlier hands. The path is that upstream stage. The attack is the altered part or code that rides into your environment inside an otherwise ordinary product.\n\nThe clinic's billing application includes a small helper library the vendor did not write. That library was changed in the supplier's repository weeks before the vendor pulled it into a release. The vendor's release signature verified, because the vendor signed what they had received. The library's own expected hash, published by a second mirror the clinic checked later, did not match the copy inside the release. Every billing workstation that installed the release inherited the altered helper. The ticket facts are the library name, the vendor signature that passed, and the upstream hash that failed. Upstream editing is what the intruder accomplished. The vendor was not the author of that change.\n\nThis is why a passed vendor signature does not close the supplier question. The signature binds the vendor to the bytes they shipped. It does not bind every earlier author. A managed provider with a remote tool is still a different path: nobody had to alter a library, because the provider could already log in. A bad update mailed directly to the clinic, failing the vendor's own signature, is the vendor-delivery problem, an impersonated or altered shipment at your door. Here the vendor did the thing you asked, and the trouble started further up the chain.\n\nDefenders ask for a software bill of materials, pin upstream versions, and compare component hashes with more than one source. They notice when a tiny helper changes without a reason in the release notes. Those are defenses. Introducing that flawed component at the supplier is what the intruder did. Removing the bad release from workstations, after you keep a copy for evidence, takes the altered library out of production. Blaming only the clinic installer misses the stage where the bytes changed. Write the component name and both hash results before you roll back, so the ticket shows an upstream miss rather than a generic failed update.\n\nStudents name the company on the invoice and stop. That company is the vendor. If the stem says the vendor shipped in good faith and the alteration happened in a library or a part the vendor got from someone else, the label moves upstream. If the stem is a remote session by the desktop partner, it is not this topic. The separator is the stage before your vendor. Trouble can be real even when the seller you pay did not intend it.\n",
        'r0': '\nA supplier alters a part or a library before your vendor sees it. The billing helper was changed in the supplier repository, then pulled into a release the vendor signed. The vendor signature verified, and a second-source hash of that helper did not. Every install of the release inherited the altered component. A passed vendor signature does not prove the upstream author was honest. A lookalike update that fails the vendor signature is a vendor-delivery problem instead. Standing remote admin belongs to the managed provider. Record the component name, the good signature, and the failed upstream hash. Rolling the billing image back removes the helper only after that pair of results is saved.\n',
        'r1': "\nTrouble can start further up than the company you pay. A supplier can change a part before your seller sees it. Your seller may ship that part in good faith. A seal from your seller can still be valid. That seal does not prove the earlier author was honest. Compare the small component with a second source. A remote admin helper is a different path. A bad package that fails your seller's seal is different too. Write down the component name and both results. The attack is the upstream change.\n",
        'r2': "\nA clinic bakery buys rolls from a shop, and the shop buys flour from a mill the clinic never visits. Someone at the mill swaps a sack before the shop opens it. The shop bakes in good faith and delivers rolls with the usual stamp. The stamp shows the shop handled the tray. It does not show the mill was honest. The clinic needs to know which mill and which sack, not only which shop stamped the bag. An upstream software or hardware part works the same way. The attack is the change made before your seller looked, and the seller's own seal does not erase that earlier step.\n",
        'r4': '\nAsk which libraries or parts inside the product your vendor did not write. Write down the component name, the version, and the hash inside the release you installed. Compare that hash with a second source and keep both values in the ticket. Do not treat a valid vendor signature as proof the upstream piece is clean. Preserve one copy of the bad release before you roll workstations back. Do not give the upstream project a support login to the clinic as a shortcut.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to name the vendor on the invoice when the vendor shipped in good faith and the change happened earlier. A failed signature on a file mailed to the clinic is the vendor-delivery neighbor. Live remote admin is the managed-provider neighbor. This attack is the upstream alteration. A valid signature from your seller shows that seller signed the bytes, not that every earlier author was clean. The second-source hash is the fact that separates those stories.\n',
    },
    'Phishing': {
        'body': 'People already act on notes that claim to be payroll, a bank, or a vendor. Phishing is fraudulent email, or a similar message, that pretends to be trusted and asks the reader to click, open a file, or type a secret. The display name can say Payroll Desk while the link host is a name the clinic does not own. Urgency and a familiar voice supply the pressure. The lie is the gap between the trusted label and the place a click actually goes.\n\nThe message is the technique, and the mailbox is only one channel that can carry it. A question may ask where the note arrived, or what the note was trying to get. A voice call that asks for the same secret is the spoken form, and it has its own name. A text on a phone is the handset form. A domain one character off a real name can sit inside the link, and that near spell is a separate fact from the message that carried the link. A page that copies colors and a logo, when the weight of the question is that copied look, is the brand neighbor. Here the message wants an action.\n\nOwen, a payroll clerk, received a note at 09:17 whose display name was Payroll Desk. The visible words said the direct deposit portal would lock at noon. The blue text read river-clinic.com. He viewed the link target in the message source and did not open it in a browser. The target host was payroll-portal.com, which the clinic does not own. Checked later from a machine with no clinic session, the page showed a lock icon because it answered on port 443, and the certificate matched the wrong host. Owen forwarded the original, headers included, to the security mailbox. The ticket holds the display name, both hosts, the noon deadline, and the fact that no password was typed.\n\nDefenders teach staff to open payroll from a bookmark. They keep the original so the header and the link survive, and they quarantine the note so a second clerk cannot click it. Quarantine is containment of that copy. It is not erasure of a program from a disk, because the harm at this stage is the message, not a file already running. The attack is the fraudulent message. A filter that tags the note and still delivers it has not ended the attack. The reader still has to refuse the click. Nobody should sign in from the message while the header is being saved.\n\nStudents name only the near spell domain, or only the copied colors, and skip the ask. They also call a live phone call by this name because a password was mentioned. If the artifact is a message that pretends to be trusted and wants a click, an open, or a secret, this is the technique. If the artifact is a voice, a handset text, or a bare domain registration, the neighbor labels apply. A polished note with no spelling errors can still be the attack. The lock icon did not turn payroll-portal.com into the clinic.\n',
        'r0': 'Port 443 and a lock icon only mean the browser encrypted the session to the host named in the bar. In the 09:17 note the display name was Payroll Desk and the blue words said river-clinic.com, while the link target was payroll-portal.com. The certificate matched that second host, which the clinic does not own. A noon lockout warning supplied the rush. Forwarding the original with headers preserves the display name, the target, and the path the message took. Quarantine contains that copy so another clerk does not click it, and quarantine is not removal of malware from a disk. A voice call and a handset text are neighboring techniques when the artifact is not this message. The bookmark, not the note, is how payroll should be opened.\n',
        'r1': 'A message that looks trusted can ask for a secret. The display name can be a lie. Friendly words can hide a different link. Read the real host before any click. A lock icon only protects the connection. It does not prove the host is yours. Open payroll from a bookmark. Save the original message with its headers. Do not type a password from that note. A phone call is a different trick. A handset text is a separate technique. A polished note can still be fraudulent. The ask to click or type is the attack.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic kitchen posts the weekly supply list on the cooler door. Most notes there are real orders from the manager. One note uses the manager greeting and asks the cook to write the storeroom code on a slip that will be taped to the alley door. The handwriting is neat. It sits beside a true order for milk, which is why a hurried cook might obey. Staff are supposed to take codes from the card in the office, not from a new slip on the cooler. A payroll message works the same way. It can ask a clerk to type a password on a page the clinic does not own, and the familiar mailbox is why that ask feels like part of the job.\n',
        'r4': 'Save the original message so the headers stay with the ticket. Write down the display name and the real link host before anyone clicks. Do not type a password, and do not open an attachment, while you are still collecting those facts. Compare the host with the payroll bookmark the staff already use. Leave the message unopened on any machine that holds an admin session. Note whether the gateway tagged the message or held it.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to name only a near spell domain, or only copied colors, when the artifact is a message that asks for a click or a secret. A live call is the voice neighbor. A handset text is the text neighbor. The lock icon means the path is encrypted to the host in the bar, which can still be the wrong host. Quarantine of the message is containment of that copy, not removal of a program from a disk. A polished message with correct spelling can still be this attack.\n',
    },
    'Vishing': {
        'body': 'A ringing phone pushes a person to answer before they compare the request with a number they already trust. Vishing is voice phishing, a fraudulent call that asks the listener to speak a secret or a code that just appeared. The caller wants the same kind of click a false letter wants, except the pressure is a live voice. Caller ID can be spoofed, so the screen may show the help desk while the voice belongs to someone else. The channel is the call. The attack is the request spoken on that call.\n\nNothing about a tone proves a role. Callers claim to be the help desk, a new finance lead, or an auditor who cannot wait. The code they want is often the six-digit value a real login just created. A common multi-factor code changes every thirty seconds, which is why the caller talks fast and will not wait for a callback. Hanging up and calling back on a number the clinic already printed is the defense. Speaking the code is the attack succeeding. A patched desk phone does not stop the push. The weakness is the skipped callback.\n\nAt 15:05 the service desk took a call whose caller ID showed extension 2100, the internal help number. The voice told Camille to read the six-digit code that had just appeared on her phone, and said her mailbox would lock if she delayed. She did not read it. She wrote the time, the claimed role, and the digits on the caller ID, then hung up and dialed 2100 from the card on her monitor. The real help desk was in a training room and had placed no call. The spoofed number and the refused code belong in the ticket together.\n\nDefenders publish a callback rule for any request for a password or a fresh code, and they back the person who refuses a loud caller. If the phone system keeps a recording, store it with the ticket. Do not argue on the line or offer part of the secret. Spoofed caller ID is part of how the call works. The defense is the hang up and the second call on a number that was known before the phone rang. That callback is not the attack. It is also not eradication of an account, because in this case no code was spoken and no session was stolen.\n\nStudents hear a secret and answer with the mailbox technique, or they answer with a text message because a phone was involved. The artifact here is the live call. A made-up tale about a failed audit can ride on a call too, and that tale is the story neighbor, while the spoken fraudulent ask is this technique. Pretending to be the help desk is the role neighbor, and it can be true at the same time without replacing the voice. The separator is the voice, and the callback that should happen before any code is spoken.\n',
        'r0': 'Caller ID can display extension 2100, the printed help number, while the voice belongs to someone else. At 15:05 Camille was told to read a six-digit code her phone had just shown. A common multi-factor code changes every thirty seconds, so the caller refused to wait. She did not speak the digits. She dialed 2100 from the card on the monitor, and the real desk had placed no call. The callback is the defense. It is not the attack, and it is not eradication, because no account had to be disabled. A mailbox note and a text bubble are different artifacts even when they mention the same clinic. Write the time, the claimed role, and the caller ID before the details fade.\n',
        'r1': 'A phone call can demand a secret. The voice may sound like the help desk. The number on the screen can be faked. A fresh code is what the caller wants. Do not read that code aloud. Hang up before you argue. Call back on a number you already had. Write the time and the claimed role. A loud tone is not identity. A mailbox lure is a separate technique. A text on a handset is another event. A patched phone does not stop the pressure. The spoken demand is the attack.\n',
        'r2': 'A warehouse keeps a wall phone by the loading dock for the dispatcher. Most calls are real drivers who are actually at the gate. One afternoon a voice uses the dispatcher first name and tells the clerk to read the gate code before a truck misses its slot. Nobody can see a face. The right habit is to leave the gate shut and to reach the dispatcher on the inside extension posted above the phone. A familiar tone through that handset is not a badge. A work call behaves the same way. Until someone calls back on a number the clinic already published, the voice on the line is not proof of who is speaking.\n',
        'r4': 'Write down the time, the claimed role, and the caller ID digits before you hang up. Do not read a password or a fresh code on that call. Call back using the extension printed on the internal card, not a number the caller just gave you. Ask the real person whether they placed the call, and record the answer. Keep any desk recording with the ticket. Do not change a password until that callback is finished.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to name the mailbox technique, or a text message, when the artifact is a live call. Spoofed caller ID does not prove the help desk is speaking. The attack is the spoken demand for a code or a password. Hanging up and dialing a known extension is the defense. A made up audit story is the pretext neighbor. A pretended help desk role is the impersonation neighbor. The callback is not eradication, and it is not the attack. A patched handset does not close this pressure.\n',
    },
    'Smishing': {
        'body': 'A text on a lock screen is built to be tapped before the reader thinks. Smishing is text-message phishing, a fraudulent bubble that asks for a tap or a secret. Carriers, banks, and delivery firms already send real notes, so a short message feels ordinary. The sender field is easy to forge, and a short numeric code can be rented by someone who does not own the brand. The link is often one character off the real carrier, or hidden behind a short address until a preview expands it. The attack is that fraudulent text.\n\nThis bubble is not the company mailbox, and it is not a live voice, even if the same person uses those paths a minute later. The clinic mail gateway never sees it, so checks that apply to mail do not apply here. A follow-up line may ask the reader to forward a code that a real login just created. The phone is then both the bait and the place the second factor is shown. Opening the carrier application from its own icon, rather than from the text, is how a person learns whether a parcel is real.\n\nJonah had no parcel in transit. At 12:14 the pharmacy phone showed a carrier text from short code 55421 demanding a fee before the evening route closed. He did not tap. The carrier application, opened from its icon, listed no held package. He photographed the sender field and the full link. He expanded the link later on a machine that was not signed into clinic mail, and the host was city-parce1.com. The carrier the clinic actually uses is city-parcel.com. The letter l had been swapped for the digit 1. The photo, the code 55421, and the absent parcel are the ticket facts.\n\nPeople should open the carrier or the bank from the installed application or from an address they type themselves. The message itself is not allowed to name the number the clerk should dial. A picture of the sender and the link is the report. Visiting the page from a phone that is already inside company mail spreads the risk onto a signed-in session. What makes this smishing is the urgent text on the phone, not a voice on a call. Refusing the tap, and checking the real application, is the defense. A warning about a new host still leaves the decision with the person who is holding the phone.\n\nStudents name only the lookalike domain and forget the channel, or they call the bubble ordinary mail because both stories can mention a package. A voice call that follows is a second event and needs its own note. If the artifact is a handset text that pretends to be a trusted sender and wants a tap or a secret, this is the name. A current phone does not remove the path. The messaging application is supposed to show texts from outside, and the sender field was never strong proof of who sent the note.\n',
        'r0': 'A five-digit short code such as 55421 can be rented, so the bubble can resemble a carrier without that carrier sending it. The 12:14 text claimed a held package and a fee due by evening, and Jonah had no parcel in transit. The link host was city-parce1.com, one character off city-parcel.com, the letter l swapped for the digit 1. That message never crosses the mail gateway, so checks on port 25 do not see it. A preview that expands a short link must not sign anyone in. The photograph of the sender and the host is the evidence. Opening the carrier application from its icon is the check. A later phone call is a second event and does not rename the bubble.\n',
        'r1': 'A text on a phone can be a trap. The sender name is easy to fake. A short numeric code can be rented. Parcel notes train people to tap fast. The link may be one character off. Do not tap a fee link. Open the carrier app from its icon. Check whether a real parcel exists. Photograph the sender and the link. Do not forward a code that just arrived. Mail filters never see this bubble. A live call is a separate event. The urgent text itself is the attack.\n',
        'r2': 'A warehouse receiving desk hangs a clipboard for carrier notes. Most lines are real delays written by the dock lead. One line says a crate is held and lists a web address one letter different from the carrier the warehouse already uses. The line is short, so a clerk might pay the fee from the note instead of walking to the dock board. The board on the wall, filled out by the warehouse itself, is the place to check. A scrap on the clipboard can borrow a familiar carrier name and still be false. A phone text behaves the same way. The safe check is the carrier app opened from its own icon, not the link inside the bubble.\n',
        'r4': 'Photograph the sender field and the full link before anyone deletes the text. Expand a shortened link only in a preview that does not sign anyone in, and write that host in the ticket. Open the carrier app from its icon and record whether a real parcel exists. Do not type a code into any page the text suggested. Leave the company mail session closed on that phone until the host is known. Do not paste the bubble into work chat.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to name only the lookalike domain, or to call the bubble ordinary mail, when the artifact is a handset text. A voice call that follows is a second event. Mail gateway checks never saw this message. The attack is the fraudulent text that asks for a tap or a secret. Opening the real carrier app is the defense. A current phone still shows outside texts, so a new handset is not a fix. Do not call a photograph of the bubble a visit to the hostile page.\n',
    },
    'Misinformation and disinformation': {
        'body': '\nFalse content steers people into bad decisions. Misinformation is false material spread without a deliberate con behind it. Someone repeats a wrong rumor because they believe it. Disinformation is a lie built on purpose so that other people will act. The two words share an outcome and differ in intent. Either one can travel by mail, by a printed sheet, or by word of mouth. The topic is the false content and what it pushes people to do, not the brand of the mailbox that happened to carry a copy.\n\nA memo showed up on the break-room printer and in mail. It said password rules had changed overnight and that staff must send their current password to passwords@clinic-help.example before noon. The intranet policy page still listed the old rules and did not mention that address. The memo was fabricated to collect passwords. That is disinformation: a deliberate lie. The same week a nurse forwarded a note that the imaging server was down. She had heard it in the hallway and believed it. The server was up. Her forward is misinformation, false and harmful, without a plan to deceive. The ticket should keep those facts apart: the forged password address, and the sincerely repeated outage rumor.\n\nImpersonation is a neighboring idea. It means pretending to be a specific person or role so a check gets skipped, such as a stranger at the lobby who claims to be the new auditor. Business email compromise uses a real or lookalike mailbox to redirect money or data, often by changing a bank account inside a finance thread. A forged policy memo that demands old passwords is this topic even if it arrived as mail. If that same thread tells accounts payable to pay a new account, the payment diversion is the money-fraud label, and you should use it for the payment. Do not use the money label for a password-policy lie that never mentions a transfer.\n\nDefenders verify surprising policy changes on the intranet page staff already use, and they teach that passwords are never sent to a new address. They ask who wrote a startling memo before anyone complies. Pulling the forged sheet and warning the staff is how you stop the lie from spreading further. The lie itself is the attack. A person who repeats a rumor they believe is still part of the harm, and that is a different intent from the person who composed the forgery. Write which of the two you are looking at before you choose a response, because a sincere forward and a crafted password trap do not deserve the same story in the ticket.\n\nStudents call every false memo impersonation, or they call a password-policy lie business email compromise because the words arrived in mail. Impersonation is the pretense of being someone. The money-fraud pattern changes where funds or records go. This topic is false content. Inside the topic, the separator is intent. A hallway rumor repeated in good faith is misinformation. A memo invented to harvest passwords is disinformation. The channel does not rename either one.\n',
        'r0': '\nMisinformation is false content spread without a deliberate lie behind it. Disinformation is a purposeful lie. The forged memo told staff to send current passwords to passwords@clinic-help.example before noon, while the intranet policy page had no such rule. That fabrication is disinformation. The nurse who forwarded a false imaging outage she believed committed misinformation. Impersonation is pretending to be a specific person. A mailbox request to change a payee is the money-fraud neighbor, not this password-policy lie. Record whether the sender intended the deception, the false instruction, and the page that contradicted it. A payee change inside a finance thread would be a different label even if the wording was also false.\n',
        'r1': '\nFalse content can push people into bad decisions. A rumor someone believes is one kind. A lie built on purpose is the other kind. The outcome can look the same. The intent is what differs. A forged password rule is a deliberate lie. A sincere wrong outage note is not that lie. Pretending to be a named person is a different trick. A request to move money is a different trick. Check surprising rules on the page you already use. Never send a password to a new address. Write down which kind of false content you found.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic bulletin board holds shift notes. Most notes are true. One morning someone pins a sheet that invents a new medication-room rule and asks nurses to leave the spare key under the blotter. That sheet was written to cause the act. Later a nurse copies a hallway comment that the lab is closed, and the lab is open. She believed the comment. Both notes are false, and only one was built as a trap. Staff should check the posted policy book before they move a key or skip a pickup. False instructions work the same way. One is a crafted lie and the other is a mistake people repeat, and the response starts by telling those two apart.\n',
        'r4': '\nSave the false memo or message and write the exact instruction it gave. Compare it with the policy page staff already use, and record the contradiction. Note whether the sender appears to have invented the claim or only repeated one they believed. Do not send passwords or change a payment because of it. Warn the people who already saw it, and keep a copy with the ticket. Do not label a sincere rumor as a crafted lie, or the reverse, until you have that evidence.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call every false note impersonation, or to call a password-policy forgery a payment fraud because it arrived in mail. Impersonation is pretending to be a person or role. Payment fraud through a business mailbox redirects money or records. This topic is false content. Misinformation is spread without a deliberate con. Disinformation is a lie built to steer people. A hallway rumor a nurse believes is the first. A fabricated demand for current passwords is the second.\n',
    },
    'Impersonation': {
        'body': 'A person who can borrow a role can borrow the obedience that goes with it. Impersonation is pretending to be a specific person or a role so the target skips verification. The pretend auditor, the pretend help lead, or the pretend courier does not need a software bug. They need a desk that treats the claim as enough. The attack is that skipped check. A verification path the pretender does not control is the defense.\n\nIn a lobby the attempt often looks like confidence plus a prop. A visitor sticker, a clipboard, or a vest can support the claim, and none of those objects prove identity. The appointment book, a callback to a number already on file, and a walk to the real desk are the checks that matter. Letting the visitor into the server room because they said the word auditor is the attack succeeding. Debating whether the vest looks expensive misses the point. The role was claimed, and the claim was not verified against anything the clinic already trusted.\n\nAt 09:12 a man at the clinic lobby told Renee, the receptionist, that he was the new auditor and needed the server room before a backup window. He wore a plain visitor sticker on which he had written the word auditor. The appointment book had no audit visit that day. The inner door log later showed no guest grant between 09:00 and 09:30. Renee did not buzz the door. She wrote the time, the claimed role, and the sticker text, and she called the audit firm on extension 4400, the number printed in the vendor book. The firm had sent no one. The empty appointment line and the closed door are the ticket facts.\n\nDefenders require a scheduled visit before anyone is walked into a sensitive room, and they confirm the visit with the host on a known extension. A visitor who cannot be matched stays in the lobby. Those rules are the defense. The attack is the false claim of a person or a role. A copied logo on a web page is a different neighbor, because that case imitates a brand rather than a human at the door. A made-up story about why the door must open is the story neighbor, and it can travel with the false role without becoming the same label.\n\nStudents answer with the fraudulent message name whenever a lie appears, or they answer with the made-up story even when the question asks who the person claimed to be. If the stem shows a claimed identity and a skipped check, this is the label. If the stem puts its weight on an invented plot, the story label fits better. The separator is the pretended person or role. A real auditor who is on the schedule, and who the firm confirms, is an ordinary visit rather than this attack.\n',
        'r0': 'At 09:12 a visitor told the receptionist he was the new auditor and needed the server room before a backup window. The sticker he wrote on himself said auditor, and the appointment book listed no audit visit that day. The inner door log showed no guest grant from 09:00 to 09:30 because the door was not opened. The callback went to extension 4400, printed in the vendor book, and the firm had sent nobody. The false role is the attack. The appointment check and that callback are the defense. An invented reason for the visit is the story neighbor. A copied logo on a page is the brand neighbor. An auditor who is scheduled and confirmed is an ordinary visit.\n',
        'r1': 'Someone may pretend to be a role you trust. A visitor sticker is not proof. A clipboard does not prove a job. The server room stays shut until you check. Look in the appointment book. Call the firm on a number you already had. Do not buzz an inner door on a claim. Write the claimed title and the clock time. A made up story is a different label. A copied logo is a different label. A real scheduled auditor is not this attack. The false claim of identity is the attack.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic building keeps the supply door locked and gives couriers a bench in the lobby. Most people on that bench really do have a delivery and a name the desk expects. One afternoon a stranger in a plain vest says he is the new safety inspector and asks to be walked into the drug room. The vest has no company mark the desk recognizes. The right habit is to leave him on the bench and to call the safety firm on the card taped inside the desk drawer. A calm voice and a vest are not a scheduled visit. Pretending to be a role at work follows the same pattern. The server room stays shut until the appointment book and a known phone number both agree.\n',
        'r4': 'Write the time, the claimed role, and the words on the visitor sticker before you do anything else. Do not open the server room and do not issue a badge that would open it. Check the appointment book and write whether a visit is listed. Call the firm on the number already printed in the vendor book, and record what they say. Leave the inner door log unchanged until that note is filed. Do not coach the visitor on what to say.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to answer with the fraudulent message name, or with the made up story, when the question asks who the person claimed to be. The attack is the pretended person or role, and the skipped check. A copied logo is the brand neighbor. An invented finding is the pretext neighbor and can share the same conversation. A real auditor on the schedule, confirmed with the firm, is not this attack. Buzzing the server room door is the attack succeeding. The callback and the appointment check are defenses.\n',
    },
    'Business email compromise': {
        'body': "\nBusiness email compromise uses a real mailbox or a lookalike address to redirect money or sensitive data. It often continues a thread people already trust. The display name can match a finance lead while the actual address is a letter or a digit off, or the real mailbox has been taken over so the infrastructure is genuine and the instructions are not. The goal is a new bank account, a swapped invoice, or a wire. A fake page that only wants a password can also arrive by mail, and that goal is credential theft. This topic is the diversion of funds or records that rides a business conversation.\n\nOwen, the clinic finance lead, had a thread with a supplier about a 48000 dollar equipment invoice. A lookalike address, using a digit in place of a letter in the clinic domain, replied on that thread and told accounts payable to send payment to a new account ending 4412. Owen's real mailbox had not sent the reply. The full address did not match the directory. Mara did not change the payment instruction. She called Owen on his extension, the number on the internal card, and he had requested no new account. The lookalike address, the account ending 4412, and the invoice amount are the ticket facts. Redirecting that payment is the attack. The callback is the defense.\n\nPayment-detail changes need a second person and a call to a number already on file, never a number written in the message. Filters may catch a lookalike display name. A truly compromised mailbox will pass those filters, because the mail is authentic infrastructure under attacker control. The out-of-band call still works in both cases. In this incident Owen's account was not the sender, so disabling Owen would have been the wrong containment. If a later case shows his mailbox itself was taken over, disabling that stolen account is containment, and it is not the same step as erasing a malicious file after evidence is kept. Do not mix those actions up inside one answer.\n\nA forged memo about password rules, with no payment, is false content rather than this money pattern. A stranger at the door pretending to be an auditor is impersonation without a mailbox. Students see the word email and answer with the generic inbox path, which is true as a channel and incomplete as the crime. The channel carried a payment diversion. If the stem's facts are a thread, a payee change, and a lookalike or a taken-over finance mailbox, this is the label. The separator is the attempt to move money or data by abusing a business conversation, not merely to collect a password on a fake page.\n",
        'r0': "\nThis fraud redirects money or data by abusing a real mailbox or a lookalike. On the supplier thread for a 48000 dollar invoice, a lookalike clinic domain asked accounts payable to pay account ending 4412. Owen's real mailbox did not send it, and a call to his known extension confirmed that. Change no bank details from the message alone. Use a second person and a number already on file. If the real mailbox had been stolen, disabling that account would be containment, not file erasure. Here the sender was a lookalike, so Owen's account stays enabled. The payee change is the attack. The callback is the defense.\n",
        'r1': '\nA business message can try to redirect a payment. The mailbox may be real and taken over. Or the address may only look real. The display name is not proof. A new bank account in a thread is a warning. Call the person on a number you already had. Do not use a number from the message. A second person should agree before money moves. A fake page that only wants a password is a different goal. A rumor about password rules is different too. Write down the full address and the new account. The attack is the redirected payment.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic bookkeeper keeps a folder of supplier invoices and a phone list taped inside the cover. Most letters in the folder are real. One letter continues a familiar conversation and asks her to pay a different account, in a handwriting close to the supplier's. The right move is not to study the handwriting until she feels sure. She calls the supplier using the number already taped in the folder, and a coworker must agree before the account on file changes. A trusted mail thread works the same way. The attack is the request to move the money, and the call on a known number is what stops it.\n",
        'r4': '\nSave the full message, including the real address, not only the display name. Write down the invoice amount and the new account that was requested. Do not change the payee and do not send the wire. Call the finance contact on the extension or number already on file, and record what they say. Require a second person for any later account change. If the real mailbox was not the sender, do not disable that employee. If it was stolen, disabling it is containment, and note it that way.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call every deceptive mail message this fraud, or to disable the finance lead when a lookalike, not his mailbox, sent the request. Credential theft on a fake page is a different goal. A forged password-policy memo with no payment is false content. Impersonation at the door has no mailbox thread. This attack redirects money or data through a business conversation. The callback on a known number is the defense. Disabling a mailbox is containment only when that mailbox was actually stolen.\n',
    },
    'Pretexting': {
        'body': 'The plot can do the work even when the listener is careful about names. Pretexting is a made-up story, told so the target will volunteer a secret or skip a step they would normally keep. The plot might be a failed audit, a stuck payment, or a record that must be repaired before a deadline. The facts inside that plot were invented. That invention is the technique, separate from whichever job title the teller also claims.\n\nA caller can describe a failed audit and ask the desk to read a password so the record can be corrected. The password is the goal. The failed audit is the pretext. If the audit system has no such finding, the story collapses. A true event told by someone who is lying about their name is a different shape: the identity is false, and the plot may still be real. Here the plot itself is fiction. Urgency and a hint of blame push the listener to repair the record instead of testing it.\n\nPaul at the service desk took a call at 11:26. The caller described audit finding 14, said the payroll share was out of compliance, and asked Paul to read the share password so the caller could fix the record before noon. Paul opened the audit log. There was no finding 14, and no open item about that share. He did not read the password. He wrote the time, the invented finding number, and the exact ask, then called his manager on the inside extension. The missing finding is the fact that shows the story was built for the call.\n\nDefenders make secret sharing depend on a ticket that already exists in the clinic system, not on a number a stranger supplies. They praise the person who checks the story and refuses. The attack is the fabricated plot that tries to collect a secret or to skip a control. Pretending to be a named auditor is the role neighbor and can happen in the same call. A fraudulent email that asks for a click is the message neighbor. The refusal is the defense. Recording the refusal as if it were the attack reverses the lesson the ticket is supposed to teach.\n\nStudents glue this label onto every lie, or they use only the role label when the question is why the listener was supposed to believe the request. If the decisive fact is an invented event, a finding number that does not exist, or a problem the caller made up, this is the technique. If the decisive fact is only who they claimed to be, the role label is the closer fit. The separator is the made-up story. A real finding that already sits in the clinic log is not a pretext, even if the caller is impatient.\n',
        'r0': 'At 11:26 a caller cited audit finding 14, claimed the payroll share was out of compliance, and asked for the share password so the record could be fixed before noon. The audit log had no finding 14 and no open item for that share. That gap shows the plot was invented. The password stayed unspoken. The manager was reached on an inside extension, and the invented number was written beside the clock time. The made-up story is the attack. A pretended auditor title on the same call is the role neighbor and does not replace this label. A fraudulent email with no invented operational tale is the message neighbor. Refusal is the defense.\n',
        'r1': 'The story itself can be the tool. A failed audit may be invented. The finding number may not exist. Check your own log before you help. Do not read a password to fix a fiction. A claimed job title is a different label. A fraudulent email is a different label. Write down the invented detail. Praise the person who refuses. A real ticket in your system is the test. Urgency is pressure, not proof. Do not create a fake record to match the caller. The fabricated plot is the attack.\n',
        'r2': 'A warehouse gate guard hears stories all shift. Most of them match a bill of lading that is already on the clipboard. One driver claims the fee was paid this morning, quotes a receipt number, and asks the guard to open the gate so the load will not spoil. The receipt number is not on the clipboard. The right habit is to leave the gate shut and to read the board, not to honor a tale that arrived with the truck. Blame and a clock make the tale feel urgent. Urgency does not create a receipt. A made up work story follows the same pattern. A caller who invents a failed audit wants a secret or a skipped step, and the missing record is what proves the plot is false.\n',
        'r4': 'Write down the invented detail, including any finding number the caller gave you. Open your own audit log and record whether that finding exists. Do not read a password, and do not skip a control, to make the story true. Do not add a fake finding so the caller number will match. Call a manager on an inside extension and record what they say. Keep the call notes with the ticket. Leave the share password unchanged.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to use only the role label when the question is why the listener was supposed to believe the request. The attack is the made up story, such as a failed audit whose finding number does not exist. Pretending to be an auditor is the impersonation neighbor and can occur on the same call. A fraudulent email is the message neighbor. Reading the password would be the attack succeeding. Checking the clinic log and refusing are defenses. Do not call that refusal the attack.\n',
    },
    'Watering-hole attacks': {
        'body': "\nA watering-hole attack compromises a site a chosen group already visits, instead of sending those people a message. The attacker studies the habit, breaks into the trusted site, and waits. When the group arrives on an ordinary bookmark, the site serves a malicious download or a script the real owner did not place there. Nobody had to click a mail lure. The trust was already in the bookmark. The attack is the poisoned site. A lookalike domain that only resembles the real name is a different trick, because the real site was never entered.\n\nCharge nurses at the clinic open the state nursing association page at about 06:50 for shift notes. That host is the real association name, saved as a bookmark. Overnight the homepage was altered, and it began offering a schedule plug-in the association does not publish. The clinic proxy log showed three browsers requesting that file from the genuine host. The association confirmed the site had been changed and that the plug-in was not theirs. The real host name, the unexpected file, the 06:50 habit, and the owner's confirmation belong in the ticket. Serving that file from the compromised page is the attack. The association removing the alteration is their repair. The clinic blocking the file and patching browsers are local defenses.\n\nA made-up story told on the phone is pretexting. A page that copies colors and a logo on a domain the clinic does not own is brand impersonation. A name one character off the real association is a mistype domain, even if the page looks convincing. Students collapse all of those into this label because someone visited a website. The separator is compromise of the place people already go. If the domain in the proxy log is exactly the bookmarked association host, you are here. If the domain is a near spelling, you are in the mistype topic, and saying the real site was watered is a factual error.\n\nDefenders notice a trusted site suddenly offering a new download, block that file, and patch the browsers the file would have met. They tell the site owner. They do not ask nurses to stop using every professional page. The attack already abused a legitimate habit. Training that says never open mail does not address a bookmark. Write the host, the new file name, and the owner's confirmation before you broaden a block, so you do not cut off the real association page for a week over a single malicious object. The malicious object is the attack. The bookmark was only the reason the nurses were there.\n",
        'r0': '\nThe attacker compromises a site the targets already visit, rather than emailing them. Charge nurses open the real state association host near 06:50. That genuine host started offering a schedule plug-in the association does not publish, and three clinic browsers requested it. The proxy log showed the bookmarked name, not a lookalike, and the association confirmed the overnight change. A one-character domain difference would be a mistype instead. Blocking the unexpected file and patching the browsers are the clinic defenses. Cleaning the homepage belongs to the site owner. Record the real host and the file name. Nurses did not need a mail lure, because the morning bookmark was already enough to reach the altered page.\n',
        'r1': '\nAttackers can poison a site people already visit. They do not have to send a message. A normal bookmark then delivers the trap. The real site was changed. A name that is almost right is a different trick. A copied logo on a strange site is different too. A made-up phone story is different too. Block the unexpected download. Patch the browser that would have opened it. Tell the site owner. Do not throw away the real bookmark forever. Write down the real site name and the new file. The attack is the poisoned page.\n',
        'r2': '\nNurses stop at the same cafe before dawn because the coffee is consistent and the door is on their route. Someone who wants those nurses does not chase them with letters. They get into the cafe and leave a bad packet beside the sugar, where hands already reach. The cafe owner did not mean to serve it. Regulars are harmed because the place was familiar, not because they went hunting for trouble. Staff should warn the owner and stop taking unasked packets from that counter. A trusted website works the same way. The attack is the compromise of a place people already open, and a lookalike storefront down the street would be a different trick.\n',
        'r4': "\nConfirm the host in the proxy log is the real bookmarked site, and write that host down. Record the new file or script name and how many clinic browsers requested it. Do not browse the altered page again from an admin machine. Block that object, tell the site owner, and keep the owner's confirmation with the ticket. Patch the browsers that could have run the file. Do not block the entire legitimate site once the bad object is identified and removed.\n",
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call a lookalike domain this attack, or to call a phone story this attack because both manipulate trust. A near-spell name means the real site was not entered. A copied brand on a domain the organization does not own is a fake storefront. A made-up story is not a compromised website. This attack changes a site the group already visits. The bookmark was the habit. The malicious download on the real host is the attack. Blocking that file is the defense.\n',
    },
    'Brand impersonation': {
        'body': 'A familiar look lowers suspicion before anyone reads the address. Brand impersonation copies a known logo, a familiar tone, and the organization name so a fake page or note feels like the real clinic. The colors match the public site. The greeting matches the usual billing voice. The host, once someone actually reads it, can still be a name the clinic does not own. The attack is that borrowed appearance. Trusting the paint and skipping the host is how the copy succeeds.\n\nThe host does not have to be one character off the real name. A near spell can ride along in a campaign, and that registration has its own label. A page can also copy the brand while using a longer name that merely contains the clinic words. The fraudulent message that asks someone to click or to type is the message technique, and it often wears this look. If the question is about the copied logo, tone, and name, this is the label. If the question is about the ask to type a secret, the message technique is the closer answer. Both can be present. The stem decides which fact is being tested.\n\nFelix in billing opened a page a coworker had pasted into chat. The banner used the clinic blue and the clinic logo, and the title read River Clinic billing. The form asked for a mailbox password. The host in the address bar was river-clinic-login.com. The real billing bookmark is billing.river-clinic.com. Those names are not one character apart. Felix did not type the password. He captured the host, the page title, and the fact that the logo matched the public site, then sent that note to security. The colors and the logo are why the page felt familiar. The host is why it was false.\n\nDefenders tell staff to reach billing from the bookmark and to read the host before any password. After the host is recorded, they can ask the registrar to take the copied page down. Those steps are the defense. The attack is the copied brand. A one-character domain is the near spell neighbor even when the page is plain and shows no logo at all. The bookmark habit is not the attack. The bookmark is how Felix avoided typing the secret into a form the clinic does not operate.\n\nStudents see a password form and answer only with the message technique, or they see a wrong host and answer only with the one-character domain, even when the host is not one character off and the logo is the point of the question. The separator is the copied logo, tone, and name. A lookalike that swaps a single character is the other label. A page the clinic actually owns, opened from the real bookmark, is not an attack merely because it asks an employee for a password.\n',
        'r0': 'A page pasted into chat used the clinic blue, the clinic logo, and the title River Clinic billing, then asked for a mailbox password. The host was river-clinic-login.com. The trusted bookmark is billing.river-clinic.com. Those strings are not one character apart, so the near spell label does not answer this stem. No password was typed. The ticket should hold the host, the title, and the match between the page logo and the public site. The borrowed look is the attack. The bookmark is the defense. If the stem instead asks what the form was trying to collect, the message technique is the closer name, and this label stays the neighbor.\n',
        'r1': 'A familiar logo can hide a strange host. Colors and tone can be copied. The name on the page can be copied too. Read the host before you type. Use the billing bookmark you already trust. A one-character name is a different label. A message that asks for a click is different too. Capture the host and the page title. Do not type a password into that form. A page from the real bookmark is different. The borrowed look is the attack. File the host before you ask anyone to remove the page.\n',
        'r2': 'A row of market stalls sits across from the clinic. Most stalls paint their own names on the awning. One stall hangs a sign that copies the clinic pharmacy name, the same blue, and the same plain lettering, over a counter the clinic does not rent. Shoppers who trust the paint walk up and hand over a prescription slip. The real pharmacy is the next door, and the door number is on the clinic map. Paint can be copied in an afternoon. The door number cannot be borrowed just by looking similar. A login page can copy a logo, a tone, and a name in the same way. The host the clinic does not own is the door number, and the bookmark is the map.\n',
        'r4': 'Copy the host from the address bar before anyone types a password. Write the page title and whether the logo and colors match the real clinic. Do not submit the form. Compare that host with the billing bookmark the staff already use. Leave the page open only long enough to capture those facts, then close it. Do not visit the host again from an admin session. Note the coworker and the chat time if the link arrived in chat.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to answer only with the message technique, or only with a one-character domain, when the logo, the tone, and the name are the point and the host is not one character off. The attack is the borrowed look. The real bookmark is the defense. A near spell registration is the typosquatting neighbor. A plain page with no copied mark, reached by a mistype, is that neighbor even if it also asks for a password. A clinic page opened from the real bookmark is not this attack.\n',
    },
    'Typosquatting': {
        'body': 'A name that is almost right is built for a hurried glance or a slipped key. Typosquatting is a lookalike domain. Someone registers a name one character off a real one, hoping a mistype or a quick look lands on their site. The clinic billing host is river-clinic.com. A registration that replaces the letter l with the digit 1 produces river-c1inic.com, which still reads as the real name if nobody slows down. Another common form writes the letter m as the letters r and n, so the shape of the word barely changes. The registration itself is the technique.\n\nThe buyer points that name at a page they control and waits for a mistype, a copied link, or a person who does not compare the characters. The page may be blank, may carry ads, or may ask for a password. The domain is this topic. The fraudulent message that sends someone there is a different technique. The copied logo on the page is the brand neighbor. A host the clinic does not own, but that is not one character off, can still be false, and it is not this narrow label. What separates them is the single character gap in a name somebody registered.\n\nIris at the service desk mistyped the billing bookmark and reached river-c1inic.com. She had not received a lure in mail or chat. The page title resembled billing, and the form asked for a mailbox password. She stopped when the certificate name did not match the bookmark she meant to open. Registration data, read from a source the clinic already trusts, showed the lookalike created the day before. She wrote both names, the creation day, and the fact that the form was not submitted. The artifact in the ticket is that pair of names.\n\nDefenders register the closest mistypes they can afford, watch for new names that differ by one character, and teach people to use bookmarks rather than memory. Blocking the lookalike at the web filter, after the name is recorded, keeps the next mistype from connecting. Those steps are the defense. The attack is the registered near spell and whatever the visitor is pushed to do there. The filter block is not a second name for the attack. It is how the clinic limits the damage. The registration note stays in the ticket after the block is added.\n\nStudents call every wrong host by this name, even when the host shares no near spell with the real one and a copied logo is doing the work. They also call the whole case a fraudulent message when the person simply mistyped a bookmark and never received a lure. If the name is one character off a real name, this is the label. If the name is unrelated and the logo is the reason it feels real, use the brand label. The single character gap is the fact that separates those stories.\n',
        'r0': 'The billing name the clinic owns is river-clinic.com. The name reached by the mistype is river-c1inic.com, the letter l replaced by the digit 1. No mail and no chat carried a lure. The lookalike registration was created the day before, according to a source the clinic already trusts. The certificate name did not match the intended bookmark, and the password form was not submitted. That pair of names, one character apart, is the evidence. The registered near spell is the attack. A copied logo on an unrelated host is the brand neighbor. A bad package from a near spell update server is the update neighbor when the artifact is the package rather than a mistyped bookmark. Write both names before any block.\n',
        'r1': 'A name can be one character off. A quick glance can miss the change. A mistype can land on the trap. Swapping the letter l for the digit one is common. Registering that name is the trick. A copied logo is a separate problem. A lure in mail is a separate problem. Use a bookmark instead of memory. Write down both names before you block anything. Do not submit a password on the lookalike. A name created the day before is a clue. The lookalike domain is the attack.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic sends patients to a building on River Street. The true sign says River Street in plain letters. Someone posts a second sign one character off, so a rushed driver reads the familiar word and turns into the wrong alley. The houses look ordinary. Nothing about the wood is dramatic. The harm is the single changed letter, which is enough to point at a door the clinic does not use. Staff tell drivers to follow the written card in the discharge packet, not a sign they only glanced at. A lookalike domain works the same way. One swapped character is the whole trap, and a bookmark is the card that keeps a mistype from winning.\n',
        'r4': 'Write both names, character by character, before you block anything. Record the day the lookalike was registered if that date is available. Do not submit a password, and do not click further on that host. Compare the name with the bookmark you meant to open. After the pair of names is in the ticket, you may block the lookalike. Do not delete the registration note when you add the block. Check whether mail or chat sent anyone there, and record that answer.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call every wrong host by this name, even when the host is not one character off and a copied logo is doing the work. That copied look is the brand neighbor. The attack here is the registered near spell name. A fraudulent message that sends a reader to the name is the phishing neighbor, and a pure mistype with no message is still this topic. Blocking the name is the defense. Do not treat a matching logo as required. A blank page on a one-character name is still the trap.\n',
    },
    'Memory injection': {
        'body': '\nMemory injection places attacker code into a process that is already running, so that process does the attacker\'s work. The process name can stay legitimate. The usual mechanisms are a write into another process followed by a new thread, a library the program never asked to load, or a started program whose real contents are replaced after launch. The person watching a task list sees a helper they recognize. The helper is executing instructions the vendor did not ship. That placement of code is the attack. It does not require the original program to have been tricked by a too-long input.\n\nA buffer overflow is a different flaw. It writes past the end of a reserved space and can change what runs next, but the defect is the missing boundary, not the separate act of opening a trusted process and depositing code. A race condition depends on timing, a fact that changes between a check and the action that relied on it. Students who memorize "memory" as one bucket will call all three injection. The separator is whether attacker code was put into a live process so the process would act for the attacker. A crash from a 900-byte name in a 64-byte field is the overflow story. A file swapped between a safety check and an open is the timing story.\n\nOn the file server, the backup helper kept the vendor\'s process name and still opened connections to 198.51.100.40. That address is not in the signed binary and not on the product\'s documented update list. Inspection showed a thread running from a memory region marked writable and executable, outside the vendor image. The firewall had allowed the traffic because the process name was trusted. The process name, the foreign address, and the extra thread are the ticket facts. Code running inside the helper, which the vendor did not write, is the attack. An allow rule based only on the process name helped the attacker hide.\n\nDefenders alert when a trusted process talks to a new destination, prefer signed images, and limit which accounts can open another process and start a thread in it. Blocking unknown libraries and treating writable executable memory as suspicious are practical controls. Those are defenses. The attack is the injected code. Killing the helper before you record the foreign address and a memory sample throws away the proof. Preserving that sample, then stopping the process and removing the launcher that performed the injection, comes after the notes exist. Do not describe the vendor helper as malicious by design. It was used.\n\nIf the stem shows a legitimate process making calls the vendor binary cannot explain, or a thread outside the signed image, this is the label. If the stem shows a too-long field crashing a service, choose the overflow. If the stem hinges on a check that was true and an action that was not, because time passed, choose the race. Patching a bounds bug is the right defense only for the bounds bug. It does not answer an attacker who already had rights to write into a running process.\n',
        'r0': "\nInjection puts attacker instructions into a running process so the process does the attacker's work. The backup helper kept its vendor name and connected to 198.51.100.40, an address absent from the signed binary and from the documented update hosts. A thread was executing in writable, executable memory outside that image. A 64-byte field overrun by a longer input is a buffer overflow instead, because the defect is the missing boundary. A check that goes stale before the action is a race and depends on timing. Record the process, the foreign address, and the extra thread before you stop the helper.\n",
        'r1': '\nAttacker code can hide inside a program that is already running. The program name can still look legitimate. The program then does work the maker never wrote. A new thread outside the signed program is a clue. A call to a strange address is a clue. A spill past a reserved space is a different bug. A bug that depends on timing is different too. Do not trust a program name alone. Write down the process and the strange address. Keep a sample before you stop the process. The attack is the code placed inside it.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic courier is known to every desk, and the desks open the door when they see that jacket. Someone steps into the jacket after the courier has already badged in and hands the courier a second package to deliver. The route and the face stay familiar. The second package was not on the manifest. Watching only the jacket misses the handoff. Staff should notice a stop the manifest does not list and should keep the strange package as evidence before they send the courier away. A trusted running program works the same way. The attack is extra instructions placed into a process people already allow, not a spill past the end of a form field.\n',
        'r4': '\nNote the process name, the user it runs as, and any address it contacted that the vendor does not document. Record whether a thread is executing outside the signed image, and keep a sample before you end the process. Do not create a firewall allow rule based only on that process name. Limit which accounts can open other processes. After the notes exist, stop the helper and find the program that performed the placement. Do not call a too-long input field this same bug.\n',
        'exam': "\nThe trap is to call every memory problem an injection. A write past the end of a reserved buffer is an overflow, and it is about length and a missing boundary. A race depends on timing between a check and a later use. Injection places attacker code into a live process so that process acts for the attacker. A trusted process name is not proof the code is the vendor's. Stopping the process before you record the foreign address destroys the evidence you needed.\n",
    },
    'Buffer overflows': {
        'body': '\nA buffer overflow writes past the end of a reserved buffer and into adjacent memory. The program asked for a fixed space, then copied more bytes than that space can hold. Whatever lived next to the buffer, a saved return address, another variable, or a function pointer, can be replaced by the extra input. The common visible result is a crash. The serious result is the program doing what the extra bytes dictate instead of what the programmer wrote. That write past the buffer is the attack. It is not a healthy recovery feature and it is not fixed by ignoring crashes.\n\nPicture a name field with room for 64 bytes. An input of 900 bytes does not politely stop at the reservation. On a stack-based overflow the saved return address sits nearby, and the crash dump can show that address overwritten with the tail of the input. The clinic badge service reserved 64 bytes for a printed name. A test string of 900 bytes, mostly the letter A, crashed the service. The dump showed the saved return address filled with those A bytes. The service listens only on the badge network, which limits who can send the input, and it does not remove the bug. The 64-byte reservation, the 900-byte input, and the overwritten return address are the facts to record. Writing past the buffer is what happened.\n\nMemory injection is a different act. It places code into a process that is already running, often by opening that process and starting a thread, and it does not require this program to have copied past the end of its own buffer. A race condition depends on timing: a check is true, the world changes, and the later action is wrong. Students who see the word memory pick injection for every crash. A too-long field and a wrecked return address are the overflow. A trusted helper making calls the vendor binary never contained is the injection. A file swapped in the gap between a scan and an open is the race.\n\nDefenders fix the write. The code must refuse or truncate before it copies past the reservation, using bounded functions or a language that enforces the length. Stack canaries, a non-executable stack, and address randomization make exploitation harder, and they are defenses layered under the bug. They do not excuse an unbounded copy. Making the buffer arbitrarily larger without checking the length is a guess that the next input will be polite. Patching the badge service is the repair. The attack remains the overflow itself. Shutting the service off can be temporary containment if it is under attack, and it is not the same as correcting the copy. Write the field size, the input length, and what the dump shows adjacent memory became, before you call the crash a mystery hardware fault.\n',
        'r0': '\nA buffer overflow writes past the end of a reserved space. The badge service kept a 64-byte name buffer and crashed on a 900-byte input. The dump showed the saved return address overwritten with the trailing A bytes of that input, which is how a stack overflow redirects the next jump. Bounded copies are the repair. Canaries, a non-executable stack, and address space randomization are extra brakes, not substitutes for the length check. Injection places code into an already running process. A race depends on timing. Record the buffer size, the input length, and the overwritten adjacent value. A 40-millisecond file swap would be a timing bug, not this length bug.\n',
        'r1': '\nA program can write past the space it reserved. Extra input spills into nearby memory. That spill can change what runs next. A crash is the common sign. The worst case redirects the program. Check the length before you write. A canary and a nonexecutable stack are extra brakes. Randomizing where code sits is another brake. Those brakes are defenses. Putting code into a running helper is a different attack. A bug that depends on timing is different too. The attack is the write past the reserved space.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic pharmacy shelf has a bin marked for sixty small bottles. A shipment of hundreds is shoved into that same bin until bottles spill into the next bin and cover its label. The next person grabs what the label no longer describes. The spill is not a clever storage method. It is a broken boundary. Staff should refuse the extra bottles before they are pushed in, not merely hope the next shipment is smaller. A program buffer works the same way. The attack is writing past the space that was reserved, and a longer bin with no one counting is not a real fix.\n',
        'r4': '\nRecord the field that crashed, the size the program reserved, and the length of the input that was sent. Save the crash dump and note whether a saved return address or neighboring value was overwritten. Do not enlarge the field in production and call that a fix before a real bounds check exists. Patch the service that performs the copy. Treat canaries and non-executable memory as extra brakes, and write down whether they were on. Do not file the crash as a timing bug.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call this injection or a race. Injection puts code into a live process. A race depends on which step wins a time gap. An overflow writes past the end of a reserved buffer, and a wrecked return address is the classic clue. Canaries, non-executable memory, and layout randomization are defenses. They do not replace a bounds check. Turning the service off can contain an active crash and is not the code repair. A bigger unchecked buffer is still an overflow waiting for a longer input.\n',
    },
    'Race conditions': {
        'body': '\nA race condition depends on timing. Two steps assume the world stays still between them, and an attacker, or even ordinary concurrency, changes the world in that gap. The time-of-check to time-of-use pattern is the form to recognize. The program checks a fact, the fact changes, and the program then acts on the fact it already checked. The check was not wrong when it happened. It was stale when it was used. Who wins is a question of scheduling, delays, and how wide the window is. A slow machine or a busy disk can make the window easier to hit. Adding memory or enlarging a buffer does not close a bug that is about order and time.\n\nThe clinic importer receives partner uploads. A scanner looks at the path report.pdf, decides the file is safe, and returns. The importer then opens that same path. In a lab trace the gap was about 40 milliseconds. During the gap a local process replaced report.pdf with a different file that kept the name. The scanner\'s verdict described the first file. The importer opened the second. The log line is the pair of events: a clean verdict at one timestamp and an open of a different hash at the next. The attack is winning that timing window. The swapped file is what you would see if you hashed both moments instead of trusting the name.\n\nA buffer overflow writes past a reserved space and is about length. Memory injection places code inside a running process. Neither requires a stale check. Students still answer overflow when a stem says the file was verified and then, a moment later, a different file was opened. The word file makes them think of documents. The decisive fact is the gap. If the harmful file was the same bytes the scanner saw, you do not have this bug. If the bytes changed between the verdict and the open, you do.\n\nDefenders remove the gap. Open the file first and perform the check on the handle you already hold, so a rename cannot redirect you. Use an atomic replace when a name must be published. Lock the directory so an untrusted user cannot swap the name, or give the scanner and the importer a private directory the attacker cannot write. Those are defenses. The attack is the change that lands between the look and the leap. A larger name buffer would not have saved the importer. Write both timestamps, both hashes, and the path that stayed constant while the contents changed. Do not "fix" it by adding a sleep, which only moves the window.\n',
        'r0': "\nA race depends on timing, including time-of-check to time-of-use. The importer's scanner marked report.pdf clean, and about 40 milliseconds later the importer opened the same path after a local process had replaced the file. The name matched. The hash did not. The defense is to check the handle you already opened, or to lock the directory so the name cannot be swapped. Enlarging a buffer does not help, because nothing overflowed. Injection is code placed in a live process, not a stale verdict. Record both timestamps and both hashes before you change the importer. A 900-byte name that overwrites a return address is the overflow neighbor, and it does not describe this stale verdict.\n",
        'r1': '\nThe result depends on which step wins a race. A check can be true and the later action false. The world changes in between. A file can be swapped after it is judged safe. A bigger input box does not fix that. Open the object and then judge the handle you hold. Or lock the name so it cannot be swapped. A write past a reserved space is a different bug. Code hidden in a running program is different too. Write down the time gap you observed. Do not add a pause and call that a repair. The attack is winning the gap.\n',
        'r2': "\nTwo clerks share one clipboard in a clinic hallway. The first clerk reads the top sheet, sees the right patient, and walks to the cabinet. In that walk the second clerk slides a different sheet under the same clip. The first clerk returns and pulls medication for the name now on top, still believing the earlier glance. The glance was honest. It was stale. A bigger clipboard does not help. The sheet has to stay in the clerk's hand from the look through the pull, or the clip has to lock. A program race works the same way. The attack depends on the change that fits between the check and the action, and timing is the whole bug.\n",
        'r4': '\nWrite both timestamps: when the check passed and when the later action ran. Hash the object at both moments and record both values if the name stayed the same. Do not insert a delay and treat the bug as closed. Change the code so the check uses the handle already opened, or lock the directory against a swap, and note which of those you recommend. Leave the vulnerable build in place until that change is tested. Do not reclassify the ticket as a too-long input.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call a stale check an overflow or an injection because the story mentions a file or a process. An overflow writes past a buffer and is about length. Injection places code in a live process. A race depends on timing. Time-of-check to time-of-use means the fact changed after it was judged and before it was used. A larger buffer is not the repair. Checking the already opened handle, or locking the name, is the repair. A pause only moves the window.\n',
    },
    'Malicious updates': {
        'body': 'Staff trust an update because it arrives on the path they were told to use. Malicious updates are a trusted update channel that was poisoned, so a path meant for a fix delivers harm. The package was altered, or the source was a lookalike standing in for the real catalog. The program being updated does not have to contain a bug. The poison sits in the channel. Installing that package is the attack. Checking who signed it, and refusing a mismatch, is the defense.\n\nA real catalog publishes a hash the clinic can compute again. The hash to compare is SHA-256. If the file hash does not match the value on the vendor portal, the bytes are not the bytes the vendor published. A lookalike server can offer a file with a familiar name and the wrong signature. A genuine server can do the same if someone changes the package after the vendor meant to publish it. A matching signature shows the expected signer covered those bytes. An unsafe setting that accepts unsigned packages is a different problem, a bad configuration rather than a poisoned file.\n\nMarco pulled the monthly desktop package from what looked like the usual catalog. The host he reached was update-cata1og.vendor.com, with the letter l in the catalog name replaced by the digit 1. The vendor portal still listed the real host and a SHA-256 value for build 14.2. The file he downloaded did not match that value, and the signature did not match the vendor key. He did not install it. He saved the file, the two hashes, the wrong host, and the portal host in the ticket. The mismatch is the evidence. The install would have been the attack. The refusal stopped it.\n\nDefenders pin the catalog address, require a signature check, and compare the hash with a value taken from the vendor portal. They keep the rejected file so the mismatch can be shown again. The attack is the poisoned channel or the substituted source. A setting that turns signature checks off is the unsafe setting neighbor. A flaw in the operating system, with a real patch still waiting, is a software weakness rather than this package. A file whose SHA-256 hash matches the portal is the real fix, and it does not belong under this label. The bad package does harm. It is not maintenance, and it does not keep machines healthy.\n\nStudents see the word update and treat every patch as hostile, including the vendor file that matches the published hash. They also see a failed check and call the check itself the attack. The check is the defense. They may call an any host database rule by this name because both feel like an operations mistake. The separator is a trusted update path whose bytes or whose server were not the real ones. Installing the mismatched file on a test machine that shares clinic passwords would still be the attack succeeding.\n',
        'r0': 'The host actually contacted was update-cata1og.vendor.com, the catalog name with the letter l replaced by the digit 1. The vendor portal still published a SHA-256 value for build 14.2 and named the real catalog host. The downloaded file matched neither that hash nor the vendor signature. The file was saved and was not installed. A later package that does match the published SHA-256 value is the real fix and leaves this topic. A setting that accepts unsigned packages is an unsafe configuration, not this poisoned file. A product flaw that still has a legitimate patch waiting is a software weakness. The attack would have been the install. The comparison that failed is the defense.\n',
        'r1': 'A trusted update path can deliver harm. The package may have been altered. The server may be a lookalike. Compare the file with the vendor value. A mismatch means you do not install. Saving the bad file preserves the proof. A real fix will match the published value. Turning checks off is a setting problem. A bug in the system is a different problem. Do not run the package to see what happens. Write down the host you actually reached. The poisoned channel is the attack.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic kitchen trusts the oven repair company that has serviced the building for years. Most visits replace a worn part with the part listed on the work order. One afternoon a person arrives through the usual service door, carries a box with the right label, and swaps the good thermostat for a different one that sticks. The label looked familiar, so the cook almost signed the sheet. The cook is supposed to match the part number on the box with the number on the work order before anything is installed. A matched number means the expected part. A mismatch means the box goes back out the door. A software update follows the same pattern. A trusted channel that delivers an altered package is the attack, and refusing the mismatch is the defense.\n',
        'r4': 'Do not install the package on a clinic machine while the signature or the hash is still unchecked. Write the host you reached, the portal host, and both hash values in the ticket. Save the rejected file so the mismatch can be checked again. Leave production on the previous known good build. Do not turn signature checks off to get the install to finish. If a test machine must open the file, pick one that shares no passwords with the clinic, and say so in the note.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat every patch as hostile, including a real vendor fix whose hash matches the published value. The attack is the poisoned channel or the substituted source. A failed signature check is the defense succeeding, not the attack. An allow rule of any is the unsafe setting neighbor. A flaw in the product, with a real fix still waiting, is a software weakness rather than this package. Installing the mismatched file would be the attack succeeding. A matching SHA-256 hash moves the story out of this topic.\n',
    },
    'Operating-system vulnerabilities': {
        'body': '\nAn operating-system vulnerability is a flaw in the platform itself, in the kernel, a core driver, or a service every process relies on. Applications inherit the risk because they stand on that platform. Patching one billing program does not close a kernel bug that lets any local user become administrator. Until the platform is patched or the host is isolated, every app on that computer shares the exposure. The flaw is the attack surface. Using the flaw to gain administrator rights is the attack. The patch, once it exists and is installed, is the defense.\n\nThe clinic file server has a kernel bug of that kind. A local account that should only restore backups can become administrator by triggering the flaw. The vendor fix is already packaged and waiting in Thursday\'s change window. It is not a zero-day. The fix exists, and the clinic has not installed it yet. The ticket should say so plainly: platform build, bulletin identifier, local-to-administrator impact, and the Thursday window. If someone already used the bug, isolating the host is containment. Installing the patch, after evidence is preserved, is eradication of the flaw. Those are different phases, and swapping the words is a common exam error.\n\nA database that executes visitor text as a query is an application bug, repaired by parameterized queries, not by a kernel package. A poisoned update that arrives from a lookalike server is a supply problem with the patch channel, not a bug in the kernel you are trying to repair. Students see "administrator" and guess every privilege story is this topic. A too-long field in one service is a buffer overflow in that service, unless the bulletin says the platform itself is at fault. Read where the flaw lives. If every program on the host inherits it, you are in this topic.\n\nDefenders inventory platform versions, install the vendor fix in the agreed window, and reduce who can log on locally while the window is open. Isolation of a host that is being exploited contains the incident. It does not replace the patch. The attack is exploitation of the platform flaw, such as a local user becoming administrator. Calling the waiting patch the attack reverses the story. Write the build, the bulletin, and whether the fix is merely pending or not yet published. A pending package means you are late. A bulletin that says no fix exists yet is a different clock, and you should not pretend Thursday\'s window contains a file the vendor has not written.\n',
        'r0': "\nA platform flaw is inherited by every application on that host until the operating system is patched or the host is isolated. On the file server a kernel bug lets a local backup account become administrator. The vendor package exists and sits in Thursday's change window, so this is a late patch, not a flaw with no fix published. Isolating an exploited host is containment. Installing the package after evidence is preserved is eradication. Parameterized queries repair a database-command bug, not a kernel bug. Record the build, the bulletin, and the Thursday window before you call the patch itself suspicious.\n",
        'r1': '\nA flaw in the platform hits every program on that computer. A local user may become an administrator. Patching one application does not close a kernel hole. The fix is a platform update, or isolation until that update. Isolating the computer is containment. Installing the fix, after you save evidence, removes the flaw. A database that obeys a search box is a different bug. A poisoned update channel is a different problem. Write down the platform version and the waiting fix. Do not call a query bug a kernel bug. A missing published fix is a different clock.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic building has a crack in the floor slab, not in one chair. Every room that uses that slab inherits the crack, including rooms whose furniture is new. Replacing a chair does not close the slab. Workers can rope off the hall until the mason comes, and that rope is a temporary limit, not a new floor. When the mason finishes, the crack itself is gone. Blaming the chair because someone tripped there confuses the furniture with the structure. A computer platform works the same way. A flaw under every program is attacked by using that flaw, and the platform fix is the defense, not another application tweak.\n',
        'r4': '\nWrite the operating-system build and the bulletin that describes the flaw. Note whether a vendor fix already exists and which change window holds it. If the host was exploited, isolate it and call that containment, and do not install the patch until evidence you need is preserved. Do not patch only the billing application and claim the kernel bug is gone. Do not treat the vendor package as the attack. If no fix has been published at all, say that explicitly instead of pointing at Thursday.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call a query bug, or a poisoned update, a platform vulnerability. A database that runs visitor text is fixed with parameterized queries. A lookalike patch source is a supply problem. This flaw lives in the operating system, so every application on the host inherits it until the platform changes. A local user becoming administrator is the example impact. Isolation is containment. Installing the existing fix after evidence is saved is eradication. A fix that has not been published yet is a different situation from a package waiting in a window.\n',
    },
    'SQL injection': {
        'body': "\nSQL injection happens when an application glues visitor input into a database statement and the database treats that input as commands. The search box was supposed to be data. A quote and extra syntax change the shape of the query, and the database obeys the new shape. The attacker did not need a kernel bug and did not need to break the database process. The application handed the attacker a way to extend the statement. That obedience is the attack. The repair is to stop building statements by concatenation. Parameterized queries, also called prepared statements, bind the visitor text as data so the database cannot parse it as SQL.\n\nThe clinic patient search logged a request whose q parameter began with a single quote, then a union that selected username and password columns from the user table, then a comment marker that threw away the rest of the original statement. The response size jumped from a normal few hundred bytes to a page large enough to hold the table. The application had built the statement by pasting the parameter between quotes. A test with a bound parameter and the same characters returned zero rows and did not change the statement text in the database log. The pasted query, the larger response, and the safe replay with a bound parameter are the ticket facts. Turning the database off would hide the symptom and would also hide the clinic. It is not the repair.\n\nCross-site scripting is the neighbor students choose because both involve hostile input and a database that stores things. Script that runs in a later reader's browser was inserted into a page as HTML. The database may have stored the comment, but the bug is the page treating it as code in the browser. Here the database engine itself executed attacker-controlled SQL. An operating-system flaw is a kernel or platform bug, fixed by a platform package, not by a prepared statement. If the stem shows a query log changing shape because of a search box, this is the label even when the stolen rows are later displayed on a screen.\n\nDefenders rewrite the query to use parameters, give the application account only the tables and verbs it needs, and keep database errors off the public page so a mistake does not draw a map. Least privilege limits the damage of a remaining mistake. It does not replace the parameter. A filter that blocks a few keywords is a partial net and will miss encodings the developer did not list. The attack is the database executing visitor text as SQL. The defense is the parameterized query. Write the original statement shape and the parameter that changed it before you take the search offline. Taking search offline can be short containment during the rewrite. It is not eradication, and it is not a reason to shut the database service down.\n",
        'r0': '\nSQL injection glues visitor input into a statement the database then executes as SQL. The patient-search log showed a q value that opened a quote, unioned username and password from the user table, and commented out the rest of the original statement. The response size jumped. A parameterized query binds that same text as data, so the engine does not parse it as commands. That binding is the repair. Shutting the database off is not the repair. Least privilege on the application account only limits damage. A browser script in a stored comment is cross-site scripting instead. Record the statement shape and the bound-parameter replay.\n',
        'r1': '\nA search box can be glued into a database command. The database then treats the visitor as the author of that command. A quote in the search can change the command. The repair is a parameterized query. The visitor text stays data. It is not parsed as a command. Turning the database off is not the repair. A script that runs in a browser is a different bug. A kernel flaw is a different bug. Write down the search text and the query shape. Least privilege limits damage and does not replace the parameter.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic librarian fills out a request slip that was supposed to contain only a book title. A patron writes a title, then adds a line that tells the clerk to copy the entire borrower list onto the slip and hand it over. The clerk who obeys every line on the slip, instead of treating the line as a title, does the patron's extra job. Closing the library would stop the slip and would also stop every honest request. The clerk should accept the title as a title and nothing else. A database search works the same way. The attack is the engine obeying visitor text as instructions, and binding that text as plain data is the defense.\n",
        'r4': "\nCopy the application statement and the exact search value from the log into the ticket. Do not shut the database service down as the fix. Mark a temporary search outage as containment only if you need it while the code changes. Rewrite the call to use a parameterized query and show that the same input no longer changes the statement. Reduce the application account's rights after that, not instead of that. Leave database error details off the public page. Do not call a browser script this same bug.\n",
        'exam': "\nThe trap is to say the fix is turning the database off, or to call this a browser script or a kernel flaw. The attack is the database executing visitor input as commands because the application glued that input into the statement. Parameterized queries bind the input as data. That is the repair. Least privilege and a keyword filter only limit damage. Taking search offline can contain the exposure briefly and is not eradication. A script that runs in another user's browser is the cross-site neighbor, even if a database stored the comment.\n",
    },
    'Cross-site scripting': {
        'body': "\nCross-site scripting lets an attacker run script in another person's browser, inside a page that person already trusts. The browser believes the script belongs to the site, so the script can read that site's session and act as the victim. In the stored form, the attacker saves the script in a comment, a name, or a ticket field. The next reader loads the page, and the site prints the saved text into the HTML as if it were part of the page. In the reflected form, the script rides in a link and comes back immediately in the response. Either way, the attack is the victim's browser executing the attacker's script. The database may have stored a comment, and storage is not the bug. The bug is the page treating untrusted text as code.\n\nThe clinic outage board has a comment box. One comment contained a script that reads the session cookie and sends it to an outside host. Nurse Elena opened the board at 09:12. Her browser then requested that outside host, and the request carried her portal session. The cookie was not marked HttpOnly, so script running on the page could see it. The comment text, Elena's request to the outside host, and the missing HttpOnly flag are the ticket facts. Parameterized queries would not have stopped this. The comment can be stored safely as data and still be dangerous if the page inserts it into HTML without encoding. The attack ran in Elena's browser, not in the database engine.\n\nSQL injection is the neighbor people reach for, because both start with hostile input. Injection changes a database statement so the engine runs attacker SQL. Here the engine did not have to change a query. The page echoed a comment as HTML, and the browser ran it. A firmware bug lives under the operating system on a device and survives a server reinstall. A script in a comment does not. Students who see that the comment was stored in a table answer with the database label. Ask where the attacker code executed. If it executed in the browser, under the site's origin, this is the label.\n\nDefenders encode output for the HTML context, send a Content-Security-Policy that limits which scripts may run, and mark session cookies HttpOnly and Secure so script cannot read them. Those are defenses. The attack is the script executing in the victim's browser. Encoding the comment on the way out is the repair for the stored case. Taking the board offline can contain the exposure while you clean stored comments, and it is not a substitute for encoding. Write the stored payload and the victim request before you delete the comment, or you will not be able to show what Elena's browser actually did.\n",
        'r0': "\nThe attacker's script runs in the victim's browser under a site the victim trusts. On the outage board a stored comment read the session cookie and sent it out. Elena opened the board at 09:12, and her browser requested the outside host with that session. The cookie lacked HttpOnly. Output encoding and a Content-Security-Policy are the page defenses. HttpOnly and Secure stop script from reading the cookie. Parameterized queries stop a database from executing visitor SQL and do not fix this echo into HTML. A reflected case puts the script in a link instead of storing it. Record the comment and the outbound request.\n",
        'r1': "\nA trusted page can run an attacker's script in the reader's browser. The script then acts as that reader. A comment can store the script for the next person. A link can bounce the script back at once. The database merely holding the comment is not the bug. The page printing it as code is the bug. Encode the text when you display it. Stop the cookie from being readable by script. A database command bug is different. A chip under the operating system is different. Write down the comment and the call the browser made. The attack is that script in the browser.\n",
        'r2': "\nA clinic waiting-room board is where nurses pin notes for one another. Most notes are plain sentences. One note is written as an order, and the next nurse follows it because it is on the board the clinic owns. The paper did not become a nurse. The reader treated the note as instructions from the clinic. Staff should copy visitor notes onto the board as quoted text, not as orders the next shift will obey. A website comment works the same way. The attack is the victim's browser obeying attacker script that was printed as part of a trusted page, and quoting that text so it cannot run is the defense.\n",
        'r4': '\nSave the stored comment or the reflected link exactly, and note which user loaded the page. Record any outbound request the browser made and whether the session cookie was HttpOnly. Do not delete the comment until that evidence is in the ticket. Encode untrusted text on output, and add a policy that limits scripts. Mark session cookies so script cannot read them. Do not call the repair a parameterized query unless the database statement itself changed shape.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call this SQL injection because a database stored the comment. SQL injection means the database executed visitor text as commands, and parameterized queries are that repair. Here the browser executed the script. Encoding on output, a script policy, and an HttpOnly cookie are the defenses. Reflected and stored differ in where the script waited, not in which program ran it. A firmware flaw survives an operating-system reinstall and is not a comment field. Deleting the comment before you save it destroys the proof.\n',
    },
    'Firmware weaknesses': {
        'body': '\nFirmware is the code in a device that sits under the operating system, or in a device that has no ordinary operating system at all. A flaw there survives a normal reinstall of the server or the workstation you thought was the patient. Cameras, disk controllers, network cards, and badge panels keep their own update images. Rebuilding the clinic server does not rewrite the camera. The attack is using the bug in that lower code: an old camera image that still accepts a known flaw, or a malicious firmware image the device was persuaded to run. The defense is a signed update meant for that device, not another pass of the server installer.\n\nThe hallway camera was on firmware 1.0.4. The vendor bulletin described an authentication bypass fixed in 1.0.9. On Monday the team reinstalled the file server from clean media because they hoped the strange camera logins were coming from the server. On Tuesday the camera still answered the bypass. Nothing about the server rebuild had touched 1.0.4. The ticket facts are the camera name, firmware 1.0.4, the bulletin for 1.0.9, and the reinstall that changed nothing. Applying the signed 1.0.9 image to the camera is the repair. Calling the server reinstall a firmware fix is how the hole stays open while everyone feels finished.\n\nCross-site scripting runs in a browser and is gone when the page is fixed. It does not live in a camera. End-of-life hardware is a maker that has stopped issuing fixes. This camera still has a 1.0.9 image, so the maker has not walked away. The weakness is firmware, and a fix exists. If the maker had ended the product and refused any further image, you would also have an end-of-life problem, and you should say both only when both are true. A script in a comment will not be solved by a camera image, and a camera bug will not be solved by encoding HTML.\n\nDefenders inventory firmware versions the way they inventory operating systems, accept only signed images from the device vendor, and assume a server rebuild left every peripheral alone. Secure boot and a check that the running image matches the signed one are defenses on gear that supports them. The attack is exploitation of the code under the operating system, or a forged image the device accepted. Write the version before and after the device update. Do not close the ticket on the server reinstall. The camera log that still shows the bypass after Monday is the proof you are in the right layer.\n',
        'r0': "\nFirmware sits under the operating system, so a server reinstall does not replace it. The hallway camera remained on 1.0.4 after Monday's clean server rebuild. The vendor fixes the authentication bypass in signed image 1.0.9. Tuesday's test still hit the bypass. That pair of versions is the evidence. A browser script is cross-site scripting and is not cleared by a camera image. If the maker will publish no further image at all, the device is also end-of-life. Here 1.0.9 exists, so the weakness is the unpatched firmware. Record both versions and the failed server rebuild. Encoding a web comment would not change the camera image.\n",
        'r1': '\nSome code lives under the operating system. Reinstalling the computer does not replace that code. A camera needs its own update. A server rebuild can leave the camera broken in the same way. Accept only a signed image from the device maker. Write the version before and after. A script in a browser is a different bug. A maker who will never publish another image is a further problem. This device still has a fix available. The attack uses the lower code. The defense is the device update, not another server install.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic elevator keeps its own controller in the shaft, separate from the computer at the lobby desk. Replacing the lobby computer, reinstalling its programs, even swapping the desk, leaves the shaft controller on the old chip. People who only fix the desk will watch the same misbehavior tomorrow. The shaft never shared the desk's programs. The technician has to load the controller's own update, the one the elevator company signs. A camera, a disk controller, or a badge panel works the same way. The attack uses the code underneath the computer you reinstalled, and the defense is that device's own signed update.\n",
        'r4': "\nRecord the device and its firmware version before you change anything. Note any server or workstation reinstall that already failed to clear the symptom. Compare the version with the vendor's fixed image and write both numbers down. Apply only a signed image meant for that model. Test the original symptom after the device update, not after another server rebuild. If the maker has no image at all, say so, and do not invent a server patch for a chip the server does not contain.\n",
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to close the ticket on an operating-system reinstall, or to call a browser script a firmware bug. Firmware is the code under the operating system, and it survives that reinstall. The camera needs its own signed image. Cross-site scripting runs in a browser. End-of-life hardware means the maker will not issue a fix. If a fixed image exists and is simply not installed, the weakness is the old firmware, not a maker who has walked away. The version before and after the device update is the proof.\n',
    },
    'End-of-life hardware': {
        'body': "\nEnd-of-life hardware is a device the maker no longer sells or fixes. New flaws stay open because no firmware or replacement part will be issued. The box can still pass traffic, open doors, or print badges. What ended is the maker's maintenance, not the device's power cord. The attack is whatever comes next against a flaw that will never be patched on that model. Isolation and replacement are how you live with it. Pretending a server rebuild updated the device, or that a patch for a newer model applies, misstates the vendor fact.\n\nThe clinic badge controller, model BX-200, sits at 10.8.5.20 on the office network. The maker ended the model in January and posted that no further firmware would be released. In May a bypass was published for BX-200. The advisory's fixed column lists later models only. BX-200 is marked no fix. The controller still answers from the office VLAN, and the doors still depend on it. The ticket should show the model, the January end date, the May advisory, the address 10.8.5.20, and the words no fix. Leaving it reachable by the whole office is how the unfixed flaw stays easy to hit. The attack surface is the abandoned controller. A firewall that allows only the badge server is a defense, not a resurrection of vendor support.\n\nLegacy hardware is the neighbor that sounds the same in conversation. Legacy gear is old equipment the business still needs, often unable to speak a modern protection mode, even when the maker still offers support. This controller is not merely old. The maker walked away. A supported camera with a firmware bug and a published image is a firmware weakness, not end of life. An unsupported operating system on a server is a platform support question. If the stem is a physical device whose maker will not ship another fix, this is the label. A zero-day on a current product still has a vendor who may patch it next week. This vendor already said they will not.\n\nDefenders segment the controller, allow only the systems that must speak to it, and plan a replacement that can be patched. They do not download a newer model's image and hope the old board accepts it. Segmentation contains the exposure. Replacement is how the unfixed model leaves the building. Write the end date and the advisory line before you argue for a budget, because the risk is a vendor fact. The attack is exploitation of a device that will not be fixed. The segment is the defense you can do this week. Do not call the device safe because the doors still open for employees.\n",
        'r0': '\nEnd-of-life hardware will not receive maker fixes. Badge controller BX-200 at 10.8.5.20 was ended in January, and the May bypass lists no firmware for that model. Later models are fixed. This one is marked no fix and still sits on the office network. Segment it so only the badge server can reach 10.8.5.20, and replace the model. A supported camera with a published image is a firmware gap, not end of life. Gear that cannot speak a modern mode but still has a vendor is the legacy neighbor. Record the end date, the model, and the no-fix line. A support contract that is still active would point at a different, older-but-maintained device.\n',
        'r1': "\nThe maker no longer fixes this device. New flaws stay open. The device can still be powered on and in use. A newer model's update does not become this model's update. Fence it so fewer machines can reach it. Plan to replace it. A device that still has a signed fix is a different problem. Old gear with a living maker can still lack a modern lock. That case is a different label. A stopped platform, rather than this box, is the system label. Write down the end date and the model. The attack is a flaw that will not be patched.\n",
        'r2': '\nA clinic still uses a door panel whose manufacturer closed the product line. The panel opens doors today. When a weakness in that panel is announced, the letter says no replacement board will be made. A board for the newer panel will not screw into this one. Staff can move the panel onto a short corridor that only the door server can enter, and they can budget a model the maker still stands behind. Leaving it in the main hallway because the doors feel normal ignores the letter. Abandoned hardware works the same way. The attack is a flaw the maker will not fix, and a fence is containment rather than a new promise from that maker.\n',
        'r4': "\nWrite the model, the maker's end date, and the advisory line that says no fix will be issued. Record the network address and which other hosts can reach it. Do not apply another model's firmware and assume it took. Limit reach to the systems that still depend on the device, and note that limit as containment. Open a replacement plan as a separate task. Do not mark the device supported because it still performs its old job.\n",
        'exam': "\nThe trap is to call every old device end of life, or to call a missing camera update end of life when a signed image exists. Legacy gear may still have a vendor and still be unable to speak a modern protection mode. A firmware bug with a published image is not a maker who has walked away. An unsupported operating system is a platform, not this badge controller. This label means the maker will not fix the hardware. Segmentation contains it. Replacement removes it. No later model's package revives a model marked no fix.\n",
    },
    'Legacy hardware': {
        'body': "\nLegacy hardware is old equipment the business still needs. The usual security problem is that it cannot speak a modern authentication or encryption mode, not that the maker has necessarily abandoned it. The device may still be sold, still have a support contract, and still be unable to join the staff wireless network without weakening that network for everyone else. The attack path is the weak mode you would have to enable, or the flat network you would have to build, to let the device work. The defense is a fence: its own segment, with only the few hosts that must talk to it, so the rest of the clinic can keep a modern lock.\n\nThe lab analyzer in hematology only supports the retired wireless mode WEP. The staff network requires WPA2. Facilities were asked to turn WEP back on for the whole building so the analyzer could associate. That request is the incident to refuse. Instead the analyzer was placed on VLAN 50, a wired segment, with a firewall that allows only the analyzer server at 10.9.50.10. The vendor still offers a support contract on the instrument. Nothing in the contract adds WPA2. The ticket facts are the WEP-only limitation, VLAN 50, the single allowed server, and the support contract that is still active. The attack would have been weakening the staff network to match the instrument. The fence is the defense.\n\nEnd-of-life hardware is the maker walking away, with an advisory that says no fix. This analyzer's maker is still answering the phone. Do not use the end-of-life label only because the device is old. Virtual machine escape is a guest breaking out of a hypervisor. Virtual machine sprawl is a pile of forgotten guests. Neither is a lab instrument that cannot do modern cryptography. A firmware bug on a camera that has a signed update is not this topic either. Here the hardware cannot take the modern lock, and the business still depends on the hardware.\n\nDefenders keep the weak device off the staff network, document why the exception exists, and revisit it when a newer instrument can replace it. They do not lower the building to WEP or to an obsolete cipher because one device asks. The exception segment should be as small as the device and its server. Write the protocol the device supports, the protocol the rest of the clinic requires, and the firewall rule that joins them. The attack path is the pressure to disable a modern control. Holding that line, and fencing the exception, is the defense. Old is not the same word as unsupported.\n",
        'r0': '\nLegacy hardware is still required and often cannot do modern authentication or encryption. The hematology analyzer supports only WEP, while the staff network requires WPA2. The vendor support contract is still active, so this is not an end-of-life model. The analyzer sits on VLAN 50, and the firewall allows only 10.9.50.10, the analyzer server. Turning WEP on for the building would spread the weak mode. A guest breaking out of a virtual machine is escape, not this instrument. Record the mode the device can speak, the mode you refused to weaken, and the single host allowed through. The maker answering the phone is what keeps this off the abandoned-hardware label.\n',
        'r1': '\nOld equipment can still be required. It may not speak a modern lock. The maker may still support it. Do not weaken the whole building to match it. Put it on its own small segment. Allow only the server that must talk to it. A maker who will issue no further fix is a different label. A guest breaking out of a virtual machine is different. A pile of forgotten virtual machines is different. Write down the weak mode and the fence. The attack path is the pressure to drop the modern lock. The fence is the defense.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic keeps one treatment lamp that cannot take the new key system. The lamp is still required for a procedure, and the lamp company still answers service calls. The wrong answer is to remove the new keys from every room so the old lamp feels at home. The right answer is a small side room whose door the lamp can open, with only the people and the cart that procedure needs. The rest of the building keeps the new keys. Aging equipment works the same way. The risk is the pressure to drop a modern control for everyone, and a small fenced exception is the defense while the business still needs the device.\n',
        'r4': '\nWrite the device, the protection mode it can speak, and the mode the rest of the network requires. Do not enable the retired mode on the staff network to make the device join. Record the segment and the exact hosts allowed to reach it. Note whether the maker still offers support, so you do not mislabel the box as abandoned. Revisit the exception when a replacement can use the modern mode. Leave the staff configuration unchanged while you document the fence.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call every old box end of life, or to call it a virtual-machine problem because the word legacy sounds broad. End of life means the maker will not fix the device. This instrument can still have a support contract and still be unable to speak a modern protection mode. Escape is a guest leaving its virtual machine. Sprawl is forgotten virtual machines. The attack path here is weakening the modern network to accommodate the device. A separate segment with a narrow allow rule is the defense.\n',
    },
    'Virtual machine escape': {
        'body': "\nVirtual machine escape breaks out of a guest into the host, or into another guest, and defeats the isolation you thought the hypervisor provided. The guest is supposed to see only its own virtual disks and its own virtual devices. A bug in the hypervisor or in one of those virtual devices can let the guest read the host's memory or disks, or reach a neighbor guest that shares the same host. The attack is that breakout. Patching the hypervisor and refusing to place untrusted guests beside sensitive ones are defenses. A guest that is merely forgotten, still running, and missing patches has not escaped anything. It is still inside its room.\n\nThe clinic test lab was running student guests on hypervisor hv-03, the same host that held the billing guest. Vendor build 8.4.2 fixes a flaw in the virtual network adapter that would allow a guest to read host disks. hv-03 was still on 8.4.1. A test guest on that host could have reached the volumes where billing disks lived. The team moved student guests to hv-09, which has no production disks, and patched hv-03 to 8.4.2 before billing returned. The ticket facts are the host name, builds 8.4.1 and 8.4.2, the shared placement, and the adapter bug. Breaking from the test guest into the host disks is the attack. The move and the patch are the defense.\n\nSprawl is the neighboring pile of forgotten guests that still run and still hold data. Legacy hardware is a physical instrument that cannot speak a modern protection mode. Neither one crosses a hypervisor boundary. Students see the word virtual and pick escape for every guest problem. Ask whether the guest left its isolation. If the harm is a demo machine nobody owns, with a default password, the guest never had to escape. If the harm is a guest reading the host, this is the label. A fully patched hypervisor with untrusted and production guests mixed together is still a risky placement, but the escape itself is the bug that makes the wall fail.\n\nDefenders patch hypervisors on a short cycle, keep test and production on separate hosts, and treat every guest as untrusted relative to the host. They do not store secrets on a host that runs hostile or student workloads. Those choices are defenses. The attack is the guest climbing out. Taking a snapshot of hv-03 before the patch preserves evidence if you suspect the bug was already used. Installing 8.4.2 after that snapshot is how you remove the flaw. Do not describe the hypervisor as the attacker. The attacker is the guest code that abuses the flaw. The hypervisor bug is the weakness that made the climb possible.\n",
        'r0': '\nEscape breaks a guest out to the host or to another guest, which defeats hypervisor isolation. hv-03 ran student guests beside billing while still on build 8.4.1. Build 8.4.2 fixes the virtual network adapter flaw that could let a guest read host disks. Student guests moved to hv-09, a host with no production disks, and hv-03 then took 8.4.2. A forgotten demo guest with a default password is sprawl, not a breakout. Record the host, both builds, and which guests shared the box. Patching the hypervisor is the repair. Separation is what keeps a future guest bug away from billing.\n',
        'r1': '\nA guest can break out of its virtual room. It may reach the host or another guest. The wall you counted on has failed. A bug in the virtual device or the supervisor can cause that. Patch the host layer. Do not mix student guests with live billing on one host. A forgotten machine that stays in its room is a different problem. Old physical gear is a different problem. Write down the host and the build. The attack is the breakout. The patch and the separation are defenses.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic rents locked study rooms off one corridor. Each student is supposed to stay in the assigned room. A faulty shared vent lets a student reach into the billing office next door and read the papers on that desk. Fixing the vent, and stopping the practice of putting students beside billing, restores the separation. A study room that was simply left unlocked all year, with old papers still inside, is a different mess. Nobody crawled through a vent. Virtual guests work the same way. The attack is a guest leaving its room for the host or a neighbor, and repairing the shared wall is the defense.\n',
        'r4': '\nRecord the hypervisor host, its build, and every guest on that host. Note whether untrusted and sensitive guests share it. If a bulletin describes a guest-to-host bug, keep a snapshot or logs before you patch, then install the fixed build. Move student or test guests off hosts that hold production disks. Do not call a forgotten powered-on guest an escape unless it crossed into the host or another guest. Write the before and after builds in the ticket.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call every virtual-machine problem an escape. Sprawl is forgotten guests that still run, still hold data, and still miss patches, without leaving their rooms. Legacy hardware is a physical device. Escape means the guest reaches the host or another guest and the isolation fails. The virtual-device or hypervisor bug is the weakness. Patching that layer and separating untrusted guests from sensitive ones are the defenses. A default password on a demo guest is not, by itself, a breakout.\n',
    },
    'Virtual machine sprawl': {
        'body': "\nVirtual machine sprawl is a pile of forgotten guests that still run, still hold data, and still miss patches. They were easy to create and easy to lose. A demo for a vendor visit, a test copy of a database, a student's server that was supposed to last a week: each one keeps an address, a disk, and whatever password it was born with. Nobody owns the patch window. The attack path is that forgotten machine. An attacker does not need a hypervisor bug. They need a guest the clinic stopped watching. Escape, the breakout into the host, is a different event. This guest can be dangerous while it stays obediently inside its virtual room.\n\nA quarterly scan found guest radiology-demo powered on, created 14 months ago, owner field blank. The local administrator password was still the platform default. The disk held a copy of a patient spreadsheet from the demo week. The inventory tag said temporary. Twenty-three other guests carried that same tag and had no shutdown date. radiology-demo's address answered on the server VLAN. The ticket facts are the guest name, the 14 months, the default password, the spreadsheet, and the count of 23 unfinished temporaries. The attack path is the unowned system. Discovering it is defense work. Leaving the default password in place is how the path stays open.\n\nA guest that reads host disks through a virtual-device bug has escaped, even if someone loves that guest and patches it. Do not file that bulletin as sprawl. Legacy hardware is a physical analyzer that cannot speak a modern wireless mode. A powered-off template that cannot be started is not sprawl in the sense that matters here, because it is not running and not holding a live service. The decisive facts are: still on, still contains data or access, and no owner is patching it. Students who like the word virtual answer escape every time. Ask whether isolation failed or whether the clinic simply forgot a computer.\n\nDefenders require an owner and an expiration before a guest is created, scan for powered-on guests with blank owners, and shut down or delete the ones nobody will sign for. They apply the same patch standard as physical servers. Those are defenses. The attack is use of the forgotten guest, often through the default password or a missing patch. Before you delete radiology-demo, copy the evidence you need: the password state, the file list, and the creation date. Deleting first feels tidy and erases the proof. Do not describe sprawl as a hypervisor feature that keeps systems healthy. It is neglect, and neglect is the exposure.\n",
        'r0': "\nSprawl is forgotten guests that remain powered on, hold data, and miss patches. radiology-demo had been up for 14 months, with a blank owner, the platform's default administrator password, and a patient spreadsheet on disk. Twenty-three other guests were tagged temporary and never given an end date. That guest answered on the server VLAN. No hypervisor breakout was required. Escape would be a guest reading host disks. Require an owner and an expiration at creation, and scan for blank owners. Preserve the password state and file list before you delete the guest. A virtual-adapter bug that reads host disks would be escape, and this demo never needed one.\n",
        'r1': '\nForgotten virtual machines are still machines. They can stay on for months. They can hold real data. They can keep a default password. Nobody is patching them. An attacker does not need to break the supervisor. Finding the owner field empty is the clue. Give every guest an owner and an end date. Turn off the ones nobody will sign for. A guest that reaches the host is a different event. Old physical equipment is a different problem. Write down the name, the age, and the password state. The exposure is the unowned system.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic basement fills with unlabeled refrigerators from old studies. Each one still runs, and a few still hold samples. No one on the current roster knows the combination, and several still use the combination printed in the original manual. The refrigerators are not climbing into the pharmacy. They are simply still plugged in. Staff should list every running unit, name an owner, and unplug the ones nobody claims, after they record what was inside. Forgotten virtual computers work the same way. The exposure is a live machine nobody watches, and a breakout into the floor above would be a different incident.\n',
        'r4': '\nList powered-on guests with a blank owner or a temporary tag and no end date. For each one, write the name, creation date, password state, and whether real data is on the disk. Do not delete the guest until those notes exist. Shut down or remove the ones nobody will own, and put an owner and an expiration on the ones you keep. Patch kept guests on the same cycle as physical servers. Do not mark the ticket as a host breakout unless the guest actually left its room.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to answer escape whenever the stem mentions a virtual machine. Escape crosses from the guest into the host or another guest. Sprawl stays inside the guest and is dangerous because the guest was forgotten: still running, still holding data, still unpatched, often with a default password. Legacy hardware is a physical device. Deleting the guest before you record its contents destroys evidence. An owner and an expiration are the preventive controls. A hypervisor patch does not, by itself, find the machines nobody listed.\n',
    },
    'Cloud-specific weaknesses': {
        'body': '\nCloud platforms add their own ways to leave an entrance open. The ones to recognize are storage left readable by anyone, roles that can do far more than the job, and a metadata service that hands temporary credentials to whatever can ask from inside the instance. These are not mysterious breaks in arithmetic. Public storage and broad roles work as the platform designed them to work. The weakness is the setting. A metadata service at the link-local address 169.254.169.254 will answer from the instance. If anything on that instance can be coaxed into requesting it, and the service does not require a session token, the answer can include keys the role holds. Requiring the token is the control that blocks the easy request.\n\nThe clinic image bucket was world-readable. A laptop that was not on the clinic network listed patient folder names with no login. The web server\'s role was allowed every storage action in the account, not only that one bucket. The same instance could request 169.254.169.254 and receive temporary keys because the metadata service was still on the older mode that does not demand a token. Three facts belong in the ticket: the public bucket, the role that says all storage actions, and metadata without a token. The attack is reading the bucket from the internet, or taking the role\'s keys through the metadata address. Blocking public access, shrinking the role, and requiring the token are defenses. None of them is "turn the cloud off."\n\nA hosted mail company that is breached is a service-provider problem. Your bucket settings were not the hole. A tampered appliance in a shipping crate is a hardware-provider problem. A generic database on an ordinary server that allows every source address is the broader misconfiguration idea, and it does not involve a metadata address or a cloud role. Students call a public bucket a zero-day. There is nothing unpatched in the sense of a vendor flaw with no fix. The platform did what a public setting asks. If the stem shows a bucket, a role, or 169.254.169.254, use this label. If the stem shows only a provider\'s outage notice, do not.\n\nDefenders turn on public-access blocks, review roles for wildcards, and set metadata to require a token so a simple request from a compromised page on the instance is not enough to mint keys. They log access that comes from outside the account. Those are defenses. The attack is the use of the open setting or the easy metadata response. Write the bucket name, the role policy, and the metadata mode before you change them, so the ticket shows what an outsider could do on the day you looked. Changing them is the repair. Calling the cloud itself defective hides the setting that was wrong.\n',
        'r0': '\nCloud weaknesses to name here are public storage, overly broad roles, and instance metadata that will hand out credentials. The image bucket was world-readable from outside the clinic. The web role allowed all storage actions, not one bucket. The instance metadata address 169.254.169.254 returned temporary keys because no session token was required. Requiring that token, blocking public access, and shrinking the role are the repairs. This is not a flaw with no vendor patch. A breached mail operator is the service-provider case. A tampered shipment is the hardware case. Record the bucket, the policy, and the metadata mode. Shutting the account down would not teach which setting was open.\n',
        'r1': '\nCloud accounts add their own open doors. A storage area can be left readable by anyone. A role can be allowed to do far too much. A metadata service can hand out keys from inside the machine. Those keys are real credentials. Require a token before metadata answers. Block public storage. Shrink the role to the one job. Turning the whole cloud off is not the repair. A mail company that is breached is a different event. A tampered device in a crate is different too. Write down the open setting before you change it. The attack uses that setting.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic moves its file room into a building where each cabinet has a switch labeled public and a badge that can open every cabinet, not only the one the clerk needs. Someone leaves the public switch on, and a passerby reads folder names from the sidewalk. The clerk's badge also opens the narcotics cabinet because nobody narrowed it. There is a wall slot that prints a temporary badge to anyone already inside who asks, unless you require a second stamp. Staff should turn the public switch off, narrow the badge, and require the stamp. Cloud storage and roles work the same way. The attack uses those open controls, and tightening them is the defense, not abandoning the building.\n",
        'r4': '\nList buckets or storage containers that the public can read, and write their names in the ticket before you block access. Record the role attached to the server and whether it allows every action or one job. Note whether the metadata service demands a token. Do not turn the account off as the repair. Require the token, block public reads, and shrink the role after the notes exist. Do not call this a missing vendor patch if the platform did what the setting requested.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call a world-readable bucket a zero-day, or to call it a mail-provider breach. No missing vendor patch is required. The platform honored a public setting, a broad role, or a metadata service that returns keys without a token. The metadata address is the clue for the credential path. A breached company that runs your mail is the service-provider neighbor. A tampered shipment is the hardware neighbor. Blocking public access, shrinking the role, and requiring the token are the defenses. Shutting the cloud down is not the repair.\n',
    },
    'Service-provider supply chain': {
        'body': "\nA service-provider weakness is a flaw or a breach in a company that runs something for you, such as mail or identity. Your records live on their systems. When they are hit, every mailbox or account that lived there is in scope, including ones your own network diagrams never drew. You often cannot patch their servers. You can reset sessions, turn on factors you control, and read the notice they owe you. The attack is the compromise of that operator, with your data in the blast radius. The contract that hired them is not the attack. The attack is the intrusion that used their platform to reach you.\n\nThe clinic's hosted mail provider sent a notice on Thursday that the tenant store had been breached and that mailbox rules could have been changed during a three-day window. Every clinic mailbox on that platform was in scope, including the finance mailbox. Forwarding rules created in the window were the artifact to hunt. One finance mailbox had a new rule sending copies to an outside address the clinic does not own. The notice, the window, and that rule are the ticket facts. The provider compromise is the attack. Removing the rule and forcing a fresh sign-in are containment of what the attacker left behind. You still cannot patch the provider's mail cluster yourself.\n\nCloud settings you chose, such as a public storage bucket or a metadata token, are a different topic. Nobody breached the mail company in that story. You left your own switch open. A hardware shipment that was tampered with before it reached the dock is the hardware-provider topic. A desktop partner who logs into your PCs with a remote tool is closer to the managed-provider label, because the access is standing administration inside your network rather than a platform they operate for many mailboxes. Students merge every partner into one answer. The separator is an operator of a service you depend on, and a breach or flaw on their side.\n\nDefenders keep a list of who holds mail and identity, require a second factor the clinic controls, and review forwarding rules and new devices when a provider notice arrives. They save the notice with the ticket. Those are defenses. The attack already happened on the provider's side. Disabling a stolen mailbox is containment. It is not eradication of the provider's intrusion, which the provider must clean on their platform. Do not tell the clinic that a bucket policy would have stopped a mail-tenant breach. Write the provider name, the window, and every rule or device that appeared in that window before you delete the rule.\n",
        'r0': '\nA service provider runs a function for you, such as mail or identity, so a breach there puts every hosted mailbox in scope. The Thursday notice covered a three-day window. The finance mailbox had a new forwarding rule to an outside address. Removing that rule and forcing sign-in again is containment. The provider must repair their own platform. A public storage bucket is a cloud setting you chose. A tampered crate is a hardware-provider issue. A remote tool on your own PCs is standing admin, not this hosted mail tenant. Record the provider, the window, and the rule. Patching a clinic file server would not have closed the tenant the clinic does not operate.\n',
        'r1': '\nA company that runs your mail can be the weak point. If they are breached, your mailboxes are in scope. You often cannot patch their servers. Look for new forwarding rules. Force people to sign in again. Turn on a second factor you control. Keep their notice with the ticket. A storage switch you left public is a different mistake. A tampered gadget in a shipment is different too. A helper who logs into your own computers is different too. Write down the window and the new rule. The attack is their breach reaching you.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic sends all of its letters through one outside post room. The room is not in the clinic building. When that room is broken into, every letter that was sitting there, including payroll, is in play. The clinic cannot install a new lock on a building it does not own. It can recall the letters, warn the recipients, and stop using a forwarding instruction someone added during the break-in. A cabinet the clinic itself left unlocked in its own hall is a different mistake. Hosted services work the same way. The attack is the break-in at the operator you depend on, and your response starts with the mail that operator held for you.\n',
        'r4': "\nSave the provider notice and write the start and end of the exposure window. List mailboxes or identity accounts that lived on that platform. Hunt for forwarding rules, new devices, and password changes inside the window, and record each one before you remove it. Force a fresh sign-in and require a second factor the clinic controls. Call removal of a stolen rule containment. Do not claim a storage-bucket change would have stopped this. Leave the provider's own repair as their task, noted separately.\n",
        'exam': "\nThe trap is to answer with a public cloud bucket, or a tampered shipment, when the notice comes from the company that runs your mail or identity. Those are different stages. A remote-admin tool inside your own network is standing access, not this hosted platform. The attack is the provider breach putting your accounts in scope. Removing a malicious forwarding rule and forcing sign-in are containment. They do not patch the provider's servers. You generally cannot. The window on the notice is the fact that sets the search.\n",
    },
    'Hardware-provider supply chain': {
        'body': "\nA hardware-provider weakness is a device that is tampered with or flawed before it ever reaches your rack. The carton can look like the order. The attack has already happened upstream, in manufacturing, in a warehouse, or in transit, and the clinic's first chance to notice is receiving. A substituted badge, an extra board, or a serial that was never on the purchase order is the path. Issuing the device, or racking it, completes the attack. Comparing serials with the order, buying through the real channel, and holding mismatches are defenses. They are not themselves the weakness.\n\nThe clinic ordered 40 access badges. Pete in receiving counted 40 units and then compared serials to the purchase order. Two serials were not on the order. One of those two was heavier than the sample badge kept from the last matching shipment. He did not issue any badge from that carton. The carton stayed sealed in the security cage. The ticket holds the purchase-order number, the two unexpected serials, and the weight note. Had those badges been handed to staff, the substituted device would have entered the building as if it were legitimate. The hold is what stopped that. The attack is the altered or substituted hardware. The serial check is the defense.\n\nA world-readable storage bucket is a cloud setting, and there is nothing to weigh. A hosted mail breach is a service operator's notice, not a carton. A library that changed inside a build is software, even when people say supply chain for all three. Students pick one supply-chain phrase and reuse it. A physical object, a serial, and an order put the case on this label. A package name and a hash in a build log do not. A provider letter about mailboxes does not either. The stage is before the device is trusted and deployed.\n\nDefenders match every serial before issue, keep tamper packaging closed until that match, and quarantine the batch when the list disagrees. They do not let an installer skip the count because the clinic is busy on a Monday. Those controls are defenses. Plugging the odd badge into a live door to see what happens would give the device the access the check was meant to deny. Write the serials and the order identifier first. Replacement from the real channel comes after the mismatch is documented. Do not describe the purchase itself as the attack. The attack is the device that is wrong before you unbox it.\n",
        'r0': '\nHardware can be tampered with or flawed before it reaches the rack. The clinic ordered 40 badges. Two serials in the carton were absent from the purchase order, and one unit was heavier than the retained sample. None of the carton was issued. The sealed cage hold is the defense. A public storage bucket has no serial to compare. A mail-tenant notice is a service-provider event. A changed library in a build log is a software-provider event. Record the order number, the unexpected serials, and the weight discrepancy before anyone tests a badge on a live door. The heavier unit stays quarantined with the rest of that carton.\n',
        'r1': '\nA device can be wrong before you open the box. The shipment may have been tampered with. Check serial numbers against the order. Compare the count with the purchase order. Do not issue badges that are not on the list. A heavier or odd unit stays in quarantine. A public storage area is a different problem. A breached mail company is a different problem. A bad software library is a different problem. Write down the serials that do not match. The attack is the substituted device. The check before issue is the defense.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic loading dock receives a crate of door badges that was supposed to match a printed list. Most crates do. This crate contains two badges the list never named, and one feels heavier than the sample in the drawer. Issuing them because the truck was expected would skip the only check the dock owns. The crate stays closed, and the odd pieces stay out of people's hands. A storage cabinet left open in the cloud, or a mail room broken into across town, is not this crate. Physical supply works the same way. The attack is a wrong device arriving as if it were the order, and matching the list before issue is the defense.\n",
        'r4': '\nCount the units and compare every serial with the purchase order before anything is issued. Write down serials that are missing or extra, and note anything that does not match the sample unit. Leave the batch sealed and unissued while you do that. Do not test a mismatched device on a live door or in a production rack. Record the order number in the ticket. Order a replacement only after the mismatch is documented, and keep the questionable units quarantined.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to answer with a public cloud bucket or a breached mail provider when the evidence is a physical shipment. Those are different providers and different artifacts. A tainted library shows up in a build log, not on a scale. This attack is a flawed or substituted device before it is deployed. Checking serials against the order is the defense. Issuing the mismatched badges would let the attack finish. There is no cloud setting to toggle and no mailbox rule to delete in this story.\n',
    },
    'Software-provider supply chain': {
        'body': "\nA software-provider weakness is a library, a build system, or an update channel that ships someone else's code under a name you already trust. The package name, the maintainer, or the build job looks legitimate. The bytes include a change the clinic did not write and did not review. Every install that pulls that version inherits it. The attack is that stranger's code riding the trusted name. Pinning a version and checking a hash from a second source are defenses. A strong signature algorithm does not answer this question if the signer is the account that published the bad build.\n\nThe clinic build server compiles the billing app and pulls helper version 4.2.1 from the public registry because the build file asks for it. Version 4.2.0 did not open network connections. Version 4.2.1 does, to an address absent from the project's notes. A second mirror's hash for the claimed 4.2.1 does not match the file the build downloaded. The package name was correct. The contents were not the contents reviewed last month. The build log, the two hashes, and the new outbound address are the ticket facts. Shipping 4.2.1 into every billing install is the attack. Stopping the build on the hash mismatch is the defense.\n\nA retired hash such as MD5, or an RSA key of only 1024 bits, is a cryptographic weakness even when the software is exactly what the author intended. A flaw the application vendor has not patched yet is a zero-day in that product, not a substituted dependency. A phone left in a taxi is a mobile loss. Students call every unexpected binary a zero-day. Ask whether a trusted name delivered foreign code. If the vendor of a boxed product signed a release that included a part they got from further upstream, that earlier stage is the supplier story. Here the clinic's own build resolved the package and inherited the change directly.\n\nDefenders pin versions, require a second source for the hash, review dependency diffs, and protect the build server as carefully as production. They do not let the pipeline float to whatever the registry calls latest. A valid signature from a compromised maintainer account is a hard case, and behavior that the previous version never had is still a reason to stop. Those reviews are defenses. The attack is the altered component under the trusted name. Keep the bad file and the build log before you purge the cache. Purging first removes the proof of what 4.2.1 actually contained.\n",
        'r0': "\nA library, build system, or update channel can ship someone else's code under a trusted name. The billing build pulled helper 4.2.1. That version opens a network connection 4.2.0 never did, and a second mirror's hash does not match the downloaded file. Every install that resolved 4.2.1 would inherit it. Pin the version and fail the build on a hash mismatch. MD5 or a 1024-bit RSA key would be a cryptographic weakness instead. A product flaw with no vendor fix yet is a zero-day. Record the package, both hashes, and the new outbound address. A vendor-signed box that merely contained an upstream part is the earlier supplier story, not this direct build pull.\n",
        'r1': "\nTrusted software can carry a stranger's change. A helper library can be altered under its real name. The build system can be the place that happens. Every install then inherits the change. Pin the version you meant to use. Check the fingerprint against a second source. A valid seal does not always mean the author was honest. A short or retired math method is a different flaw. A bug with no fix published yet is different. A lost phone is different. Write down the package name and the version. The attack is the stranger's code in the trusted name.\n",
        'r2': "\nA clinic kitchen follows a recipe card that calls for a jar from a brand everyone knows. The jar on the shelf has the right label and the right version stamp. Someone refilled it, and the powder is not what the last jar held. Every tray baked from that card inherits the refill. The cook should have matched the jar to a sealed sample, not trusted the label alone. A short, worn-out measuring method is a different problem from a refilled jar. A trusted software name works the same way. The attack is a stranger's change delivered under that name, and checking a second sample before you bake is the defense.\n",
        'r4': '\nWrite the package name, the version the build resolved, and the hash it downloaded. Compare that hash with a second source and keep both values. Do not ship the build while the hashes disagree. Note any new network destination the version opens that the previous version did not. Protect the build server, and pin versions instead of floating to whatever is newest. Keep a copy of the bad file before you clear the cache. Do not call a retired signature method this same event.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call a tainted library a zero-day or a weak hash. A zero-day has no vendor patch yet. A retired method is a cryptographic problem even when the bytes are what the author sent. A lost phone is a mobile issue. This attack is foreign code under a trusted library, build, or update name. A mismatched second hash is the clue. Pinning the known version is the defense.\n',
    },
    'Cryptographic weaknesses': {
        'body': "\nA cryptographic weakness is a broken or retired method, a key that is too short, or a secret that was reused or leaked. Hashing is not encryption. SHA-256 is the hash to require when you are teaching a signature or an integrity check. A partner who asks for MD5 or SHA-1 on a signature is asking you to trust a hash that no longer resists collisions. RSA 2048 is a common minimum for that algorithm. A 1024-bit RSA key is below that minimum. ECDSA on the P-256 curve is a common modern signature choice. The attack is forging a signature because the hash collides, recovering or guessing a short key, or using a secret that is no longer secret. Refusing the weak option is the defense.\n\nThe partner portal offered MD5 as the signature hash on the eligibility file. The clinic refused and named SHA-256 as the requirement. A second interface presented an RSA public key of 1024 bits for the same exchange. The clinic required 2048, or an elliptic-curve signature on P-256. Nothing in the firewall was mis-set. The offered math was the problem. The ticket records the string MD5, the key size 1024, and the refusal. Trusting the MD5 signature would have been the attack's opportunity. The refusal is the defense. Calling MD5 a form of encryption would also be a factual error, because that function is a hash.\n\nA library that was altered under a trusted name can be signed with SHA-256 and still be malicious. The algorithm worked. The bytes were the problem, and that is the software-provider topic. A product bug with no vendor patch yet is a zero-day, even if the product uses strong cryptography everywhere else. A phone with no screen lock is a mobile weakness, even when its mail connection uses a current suite. Students label every incident a crypto failure because the word sounds broad. The separator is the method, the key length, or the secrecy of the key. If those three are sound and the file contents were substituted, you are not here.\n\nDefenders publish a short allow list: SHA-256 for the hash in this lesson, RSA at 2048 or higher when RSA is required, and P-256 for the elliptic-curve case you expect partners to support. They reject MD5 and SHA-1 for signatures. They rotate secrets that were reused or found in a repository. Those rules are defenses. The attack uses the weak math or the leaked secret. Write the offered algorithm name and the offered key size before you agree to an exception. An exception that allows MD5 for a week is a decision to accept the weakness, not a workaround that removes it.\n",
        'r0': '\nA cryptographic weakness is a retired or broken method, a short key, or a reused or leaked secret. Hashing is not encryption. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collisions and are refused for signatures. SHA-256 is the hash to require. RSA 2048 is a common minimum, so a 1024-bit RSA key is short. ECDSA on P-256 is a common modern signature choice. The partner offered MD5 and a 1024-bit RSA key. The clinic refused both. A SHA-256 signature on a tainted library is a software-provider problem, not a weak algorithm. Record the offered name and the offered size. Rotation is the follow-up only when the secret itself has leaked or been reused.\n',
        'r1': '\nThe math or the secret can be too weak. A retired fingerprint method should be refused. A key that is too short should be refused. A hash is not a cipher. A leaked secret is no longer a secret. A secret reused in many places fails together. Require the current strong fingerprint. A tainted package can still carry a strong seal. That is a different problem. A flaw with no patch yet is different. A missing phone lock is different. Write down the method and the size that were offered. The attack uses the weak math or the leaked secret.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic safe still has a wax seal that street stalls can copy with a mold, and a padlock with so few pins a patient could rake it open. Finance refuses both. They ask for a seal the street cannot duplicate and a lock with enough pins to matter. Nobody calls the wax seal a lock, and nobody calls the lock a seal. A jar that was refilled under a trusted label is a different kind of trouble from a weak seal. Weak methods and short or leaked secrets work the same way. The attack uses that weakness, and refusing the weak option is the defense.\n',
        'r4': '\nWrite down the exact method and key size the partner offered. Do not accept a retired hash for a signature, and do not call that hash encryption. Require the strong hash you were taught, and a key that meets the minimum size. Refuse the exchange until the offer changes, and note the refusal in the ticket. If a secret was found in a repository or reused widely, treat it as exposed and plan a rotation. Do not file a tainted but strongly signed library under this label.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to treat a hash as encryption, or to call altered code a weak algorithm because a signature existed. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collisions. SHA-256 is the hash to require. RSA 2048 is a common minimum, and 1024 bits is short. The attack uses the weak method, the short key, or a leaked secret. Altered code with a strong signature is the software neighbor. Refusal is the defense.\n',
    },
    'Misconfiguration': {
        'body': 'A product can behave exactly as its maker designed and still be unsafe in your building. Misconfiguration is an unsafe setting on a system that still works as designed. It is not a software bug. The chosen setting is wrong for the environment. A database that allows any host to connect, because the allow rule says any, is the pattern to remember. Nothing in the engine is broken. The rule means what it says, and what it says is too wide for a billing store that should serve only its own application.\n\nYou confirm the case by reading the live setting beside the standard the clinic adopted. The evidence is an allow line that says any, a management page bound to the whole network, or logging turned off so the next incident has no record. A template can stamp the same loose rule onto every new database, and that return of the bad setting is still this topic. A safe choice already exists in the vendor guide, so this is not a flaw with no patch yet. It is also not the password printed on a sticker. The sticker secret and the any host rule answer different questions, and either one can exist without the other.\n\nThe billing database at 10.4.8.21 listens on TCP 5432. The allow rule is any, so a host that can route to that port may attempt a login. The clinic baseline names only the application hosts 10.4.8.30 and 10.4.8.31. Lena wrote both the live rule and the baseline rule into the ticket before she changed anything. The engine answered exactly as the any rule requires. No crash and no exploit of a code flaw were required. The unsafe behavior is the setting. Limiting the rule to those two hosts is the defense, and it is not a patch to the database product.\n\nDefenders keep a baseline, review rules before a database is exposed, and watch for drift when a template runs again. They turn on the logs the standard requires. The exposure an attacker would use is a connection the loose setting permits. Do not invent a code fix for an engine that is doing what it was told. Narrowing the rule is containment of that exposure. It is not the same step as deleting malware after evidence is saved, because there may be no malware on the host at all. Write the live value and the required value first so the person who edits the rule is not guessing.\n\nStudents call this a code defect, or a flaw with no patch yet, when the vendor guide already shows a tighter setting. They call it a shipped password when the password is unique and the allow rule is the problem. An open listener on a public address is the neighboring port story when the question is simply that the port answers. Here the decisive fact is the any source choice on a system that works as designed. An upgrade that keeps the same any rule does not fix it. The setting itself has to change.\n',
        'r0': 'The billing database at 10.4.8.21 listens on TCP 5432 with an allow rule of any, so any host that can route there may try a login. The product is behaving as that rule is documented to behave. The baseline names only 10.4.8.30 and 10.4.8.31. Both the live rule and the baseline were written down before a change. No code flaw had to be exploited, and the vendor guide already shows the tighter choice, so this is not a flaw waiting on a patch. A published sticker password would be a different fact. After the note exists, narrowing the rule contains the exposure. That change does not delete malware, and an upgrade that leaves the any rule in place does not correct the setting.\n',
        'r1': 'The system can work as designed and still be unsafe. The setting is what is wrong. An allow rule may say any host. Any host that can reach it may try to connect. Change the setting after you record it. Do not invent a code patch. Compare the live rule with the baseline. A shipped password is a different problem. A flaw with no patch yet is different. A template can bring the loose rule back. Write the required value beside the live value. The wide setting is the exposure.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic medicine room has a lock that works exactly as the locksmith built it. The bolt throws, the key turns, and the mechanism is not broken. The lock was hung so the public hallway is the side that opens, and the staff side needs a key the nurses do not carry during a rush. Anyone in the hall can walk in. The room is doing what the install told it to do. The failure is which way the lock faces, and a working bolt does not make that choice safe. Staff should note which way the lock faces before they move it. An unsafe setting on a working database follows the same pattern. The engine is faithful to a rule that says any host may connect, and changing that rule is the defense.\n',
        'r4': 'Write the live allow rule and the baseline rule side by side before you change either one. Record the database address and the port that is listening. Do not invent a software patch for an engine that is doing what the setting says. After the note is in the ticket, limit the rule to the application hosts the baseline names. Turn on the logs the standard requires, and do not disable them to save disk. Check the template that builds new databases so the loose rule does not return on the next deploy.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call an any host database rule a code defect, or a flaw with no patch yet, when the vendor guide already shows a tighter setting. A shipped password is the default credential neighbor. A public listener is the open port neighbor when the question is that the port answers at all. Here the decisive fact is the any source choice. Changing that setting is the defense. An upgrade that keeps the same any rule does not fix it. Narrowing the rule is containment of the exposure, not deletion of malware.\n',
    },
    'Mobile-device weaknesses': {
        'body': "\nMobile weaknesses come from the fact that the device is a computer that leaves the building every night. Loss, a missing or weak screen lock, sideloaded apps, and mail or files stored on an unmanaged phone are the usual shape. The phone can be fully patched and still be the incident. The attack, or the loss that an attacker would use, depends on physical possession plus weak controls, not on a vendor bulletin. A zero-day has no patch yet and is a different clock. A retired hash is a cryptographic problem. A tainted server library is a build problem. This topic is the pocket computer.\n\nDr. Ibarra's phone had no screen lock. The native mail app held the clinic mailbox. At 18:40 the phone was left in a taxi. It had never been enrolled in management, so there was no remote wipe. The loss was noticed at 21:05, and the mail session was still valid. The ticket facts are the absent lock, the mail profile, the taxi, the two times, and the missing enrollment. Whoever held the phone could open mail without a secret. That exposure is the weakness being realized. Enrolling the fleet, requiring a lock, turning on device encryption, and being able to wipe are defenses. They were not in place at 18:40.\n\nSideloading is the other common door. An app installed from a file, outside the store the clinic trusts, can run with access to mail and files the store copy would not have been granted. Blocking sideload is a defense. It does not describe the taxi loss, and the taxi loss does not require a sideloaded app. Mention both so a stem about an outside installer is not forced into the lost-phone story, and a stem about a taxi is not forced into an app-store story. The shared idea is a computer the clinic does not physically control after hours.\n\nDefenders enroll devices before mail is allowed, require a screen lock, encrypt the device, and keep remote wipe ready. They prohibit sideload except through a process they can revoke. After a loss, a wipe and a mail-token revocation are containment of the data and the session on that device. They are not the same step as deleting malware from a server after evidence is saved, and they do not patch a product flaw that has no fix yet. Write the lock state and the enrollment state before you wipe, if you can still reach the phone. If you cannot, write that the wipe was impossible and revoke the mail token anyway.\n",
        'r0': "\nMobile weaknesses include loss, missing screen locks, sideloaded apps, and devices that leave controlled space every night. Dr. Ibarra's phone had no lock, held clinic mail, and was left in a taxi at 18:40. It was not enrolled, so no remote wipe existed. The loss was noticed at 21:05 with the mail session still valid. Require enrollment, a lock, encryption, and wipe before mail is allowed, and block sideload. A flaw with no vendor patch yet is a zero-day, not this taxi. A retired hash is a cryptographic issue. Record the lock, the times, and the enrollment gap. Sideload is the same family of weakness when the stem is an installer from outside the store.\n",
        'r1': '\nA phone is a computer that leaves the building. A missing screen lock exposes the mail on it. A lost phone in a taxi is a real incident. Sideloaded apps skip the store checks. Remote wipe only works if the phone was enrolled. Require a lock and encryption. Enroll the phone before it stores mail. A flaw with no patch yet is a different problem. A weak signature method is a different problem. A bad library on a server is a different problem. Write down the lock state and whether wipe was possible. The exposure is the pocket computer.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic doctor carries a chart folder home every evening. The folder has no clasp. One night it is left in a cab. Anyone who picks it up can read the notes, because nothing asked for a second check. A folder that was enrolled in a checkout desk could have been flagged and its spare copies pulled. A broken formula in the pharmacy safe is a different weakness from a missing clasp. A chart that was never supposed to leave is not the same as a formula. Pocket computers work the same way. The exposure is a device that leaves with mail and no lock, and enrollment with a required lock is the defense that was missing.\n',
        'r4': '\nWrite whether the phone had a screen lock, whether mail was present, and where it was lost. Record the times of the loss and of the discovery. Check whether management enrollment and remote wipe were even possible, and write that answer before you depend on a wipe. Revoke the mail session if the phone cannot be wiped. Do not allow sideloaded apps onto enrolled devices while you handle the loss. Do not call the incident a missing vendor patch if the phone was current and simply unlocked.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to call a taxi loss a zero-day or a broken cipher. A zero-day has no vendor patch yet. A retired hash is a cryptographic weakness. A tainted server library is a software-provider issue. This exposure is the phone: no lock, mail on the device, no enrollment, and a life outside the building. Sideload is the related door when the app came from outside the store. Wipe and token revocation contain the loss.\n',
    },
    'Zero-day vulnerabilities': {
        'body': "\nA zero-day is a flaw the vendor has not patched yet. No fix exists to install. Signature-only detection can miss it, because a signature often appears after the flaw is public and sometimes not even then. Other controls have to carry the load: reduce who can reach the service, watch for the behavior the notice describes, and be ready to apply a fix the day it is published. The attack is exploitation of a hole that still has no vendor repair. Isolation is containment. It is not eradication, and it is not a patch. Calling Monday's workaround a patch teaches the wrong clock.\n\nOn Monday the clinic VPN gateway had a newly announced bug. The vendor advisory said a fix was not available. The intrusion system had no signature for it. The team stopped offering the gateway to the whole internet and allowed only the clinic's known address ranges. They watched logs for the behavior the advisory named. They did not tell leadership the gateway was patched. On Friday the vendor published the fix. Until Friday the condition was a zero-day. Saving logs and then installing Friday's package, once it existed, is eradication of that flaw. Monday's narrowing of reach was containment. Those words have to match. A student who calls Monday's firewall change eradication has skipped the phase list.\n\nA supported server that is late on a package already in Thursday's window is not a zero-day. The fix exists and the clinic has not installed it. An unsupported product, or end-of-life hardware marked no fix, may never get a patch. A zero-day is usually on something the vendor still maintains, and the missing piece is time, not a closed product line. A retired hash is a cryptographic choice, not an unpatched product bug. A tainted library is foreign code in a trusted build. A phone in a taxi can be fully patched. If the stem says the vendor has no fix yet, this is the label. The moment the fix ships, the zero-day name stops fitting, and delay after that is ordinary lateness.\n\nDefenders keep the service reachable by as few parties as possible while they wait, prefer behavior and allow-lists over a signature that does not exist, and subscribe to the vendor so Friday is not a surprise. They do not invent a fictional patch to calm a meeting. The attack is use of the unpatched flaw. The temporary restriction is the defense available today. Write the advisory date, the sentence that says no fix is available, and the exact restriction you applied. When the package appears, record that the label has changed and install it after the evidence you still need is stored.\n",
        'r0': '\nA zero-day has no vendor patch yet, so signature-only tools can miss it and other controls must carry the load. On Monday the VPN gateway advisory said a fix was not available. The team limited the gateway to known clinic address ranges and did not claim it was patched. That limit is containment. On Friday the vendor published the fix. Installing it after logs are saved is eradication, and only then does the zero-day name stop applying. A package already waiting in a change window is ordinary lateness. A retired hash is not this clock. Record the no-fix sentence and both dates.\n',
        'r1': "\nA flaw can exist before the maker ships a fix. No patch is available yet. Signature tools can miss it. Other controls have to carry the load. Cut off how far the service can be reached. Watch for the behavior in the notice. Do not say the issue is patched. A fix that is merely not installed yet is different. A retired math method is different. A lost phone is different. A stranger's code in a trusted package is different. Write down that no fix exists. Isolation is containment until a fix arrives.\n",
        'r2': '\nA clinic door uses a lock the locksmith has not built a replacement for yet. The door still has to be a door. Staff do not tell the morning meeting that a new lock is installed. They narrow who is allowed through and they watch the hall. When the replacement arrives on a later day, then they change the hardware, and they keep the old log. Pretending the part came early would be a different story from waiting honestly. An unpatched product flaw works the same way. There is no vendor fix yet, and reducing how far the service can be reached is the defense that exists today.\n',
        'r4': '\nWrite the advisory date and the vendor sentence that says no fix is available. Do not enter a patch date you do not have. Record how you reduced reach, and call that containment, not removal of the flaw. Watch for the behavior the advisory describes and keep those logs. When a fix is actually published, save the evidence you still need and only then install it. Note that the no-patch condition ended on that later date. Do not use this label for a package that was already sitting in a change window.\n',
        'exam': '\nThe trap is to say the fix is already waiting, or to call isolation eradication. A zero-day has no patch yet. Signature-only defenses can miss it. Narrowing reach is containment. Installing a package is possible only after the vendor publishes one, and that later install, after evidence is saved, is eradication. A package already in a change window is lateness. Do not claim the service is patched on the day the advisory says no fix exists.\n',
    },
}
